// Generated by createPages.xsl - the documentation's sections, searched by search.js
window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"page":"Overview","section":"","url":"index.html","text":"DeltaXignia's XSLT\/XPath extension for Visual Studio Code supports XSLT 3.0 and XPath 3.1, and the XSLT 4.0 and XPath 4.0 features implemented in Saxon 13. The aim is to make writing, reviewing and debugging XSLT easier. The extension understands each XPath expression - which names are variables, functions or node tests, and where each is declared, including in imported modules - so problems show as you type, and you can go from any name to its declaration, rename it safely, or step through the expression in the debugger. For what's new in the latest release, see What's New."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Getting started","url":"index.html#getting-started","text":"Install the extension and open an XSLT file - editing, highlighting and checking need no setup. To run or debug stylesheets, set XSLT.tasks.saxonJar to the path of your Saxon jar (Saxon-HE is fine), with a Java runtime on the PATH - see The Saxon jar. Press ⌘⌥R (Ctrl+Alt+R on Windows\/Linux) to Quick Run the stylesheet with the XML file you last had open - shown in the status bar, where you can pick another."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Writing XSLT","url":"index.html#writing","text":"Problems are reported as you type - in XPath syntax, XSLT instructions, types and function calls, and references to declarations in other modules - many with a quick fix. Auto-completion offers the variables, functions, templates and modes in scope, and node names from the XML file you're working with. See Editing XSLT\/XPath. An xsl:switch that doesn't cover an enumeration's values, with quick fixes to add the missing cases"},{"page":"Overview","section":"Reviewing XSLT","url":"index.html#reviewing","text":"Hovering over a name shows its declaration - its type, and its documentation from an xsl:note - wherever it's declared. Go to Definition, Find References, the Outline and the XSLT Imports view help you find your way around a large stylesheet and its modules. See Code Navigation and Code Documentation. Hovering over a record field in a lookup: its type, documentation and allowed values"},{"page":"Overview","section":"Running XSLT","url":"index.html#running","text":"Quick Run runs the stylesheet in the active editor with SaxonJ, SaxonJS or SaxonC. The first run creates a task in tasks.json, which you can edit - and you're asked for the values of any required stylesheet parameters. Saxon's errors are listed in the Problems view, linked to the code. See Quick Run. Quick Run uses the stylesheet in the active editor (1), the processor (2) and the XML context file (3), shown in the status bar"},{"page":"Overview","section":"Debugging XSLT","url":"index.html#debugging","text":"The debugger sets breakpoints and steps through templates, functions and multi-line XPath expressions, showing variables, the context item and the output written so far. Where Saxon's standard tracing stops at XSLT instructions, this debugger uses Saxon's own APIs to inject extra trace points and re-parse XPath to locate sub-expressions, so it can stop deep inside multi-line expressions and inline functions. See XSLT Debugger. Paused within an XPath expression, with its range variable, the context, global variables, inline values and the call stack"},{"page":"Overview","section":"Scope","url":"index.html#scope","text":"The extension supports the XSLT 4.0 and XPath 4.0 features that Saxon 13 implements. Its checks are made as you type, without compiling the stylesheet - Saxon reports anything else when the stylesheet runs. Schema-aware features aren't supported. The debugger needs SaxonJ 12 or later (any edition); SaxonJS and SaxonC stylesheets can be run, but not debugged."},{"page":"Overview","section":"New to Visual Studio Code?","url":"index.html#new-to-vs-code","text":"These pages complement the extensive Visual Studio Code documentation that begins: Visual Studio Code is a lightweight but powerful source code editor which runs on your desktop and is available for Windows, macOS and Linux. It comes with built-in support for JavaScript, TypeScript and Node.js and has a rich ecosystem of extensions for other languages..."},{"page":"Overview","section":"The User Interface","url":"index.html#the-user-interface","text":"Visual Studio Code's user interface has a different look and feel to that of legacy XSLT editors. The Command Palette, along with keybindings to most common commands, play a significant role in providing a clearer environment to work in. User interface clutter is significantly reduced by eschewing previous designs which depended on a set of toolbars, each with an abundance of buttons. The application menu bar and associated dropdown menus usefully group the more essential actions for managing your code. The View menu is particularly useful as it logically sets out the large set of view-configuration commands. The sections below introduce you to the fundamental parts of the user interface from the perspective of an XSLT\/XPath developer."},{"page":"Overview","section":"The Command Palette","url":"index.html#the-command-palette","text":"A principal part of Visual Studio Code's user interface is the Command Palette, triggered by the keyboard shortcut ⇧⌘P. The Command Palette shows all available commands in a dropdown list. The list of commands is filtered as you type. The most recently used commands are shown at the top of the list."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Opening Workspace Files","url":"index.html#opening-workspace-files","text":"The File: Open Folder command is used to select the starting 'Workspace' folder to be used by VS Code. More folders can be added to the Workspace subsequently using the Workspaces: Add folder to workspace command. The keyboard shortcut ⌘P provides quick-access to all Workspace files from a pick-list. 'Fuzzy-matching' allows you to type in just a part of the filename to filter the pick-list files."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Keyboard Shortcuts","url":"index.html#keyboard-shortcuts","text":"While the Command Palette provides convenient access to features you should find that keyboard shortcuts are more effective for commands you use most frequently. The keyboard shortcut for commands is shown alongside each listed command in the Command Palette. Not all commands have key bindings, but these can be configured using the command Preferences: Open Keyboard Shortcuts (⌘K⌘S). See: Key Bindings for Visual Studio Code"},{"page":"Overview","section":"Settings","url":"index.html#settings","text":"By adding or modifying Visual Studio Settings, using the Preferences: Open User Settings command, you can customize a range of editor and extension features to meet your needs. See the Settings section for details on some of the more important settings for developing in XSLT and XPath."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Install the XSLT\/XPath Extension","url":"index.html#install-the-xslt-xpath-extension","text":"Extensions are managed in the Extensions view (⇧⌘X) of the Side Bar. Type XSLT XPath in the Extensions view search bar and, on the XSLT\/Path for Visual Studio Code entry, press the install button ."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Color Theme Extensions","url":"index.html#color-theme-extensions","text":"Visual Studio Code includes a small set of light and dark color themes. Alternative color themes are installed as extensions. The number of available themes can be overwhelming, but VS Code has a built-in Theme Preview feature and there are websites like vscodethemes that provide a preview before installing the theme. The XSLT Dark Themes extension provides a set of popular dark color themes specially enhanced for XSLT and XPath The XSLT\/XPath extension syntax highlighter uses Semantic Highlighting that should be enabled specifically in Settings to work with external color themes. Highlighter tokens are defined to provide effective highlighting for all the common color themes."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Other Extensions","url":"index.html#other-extensions","text":"Among the hundreds of high quality extensions available for Visual Studio Code, Live Share, for collaborative working, is particularly useful."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Viewing HTML Output","url":"index.html#viewing-html-output","text":"When your XSLT generates HTML, you can view the result in Visual Studio Code's integrated browser, with no extension needed. When a transform writes an HTML result file, the notification that the task has completed has an Open in Browser button, as well as Open for the file's source. You can also choose Open in Integrated Browser from the editor title bar of an HTML file, or from its context menu in the Explorer. With the workbench.browser.autoReloadOnFileChange setting, the page reloads each time the transform writes the result file again - so you can edit the stylesheet, run it with Quick Run, and see the updated output alongside the XSLT."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Edit XML, XSLT or XPath","url":"index.html#edit-xml-xslt-or-xpath","text":"For a comprehensive guide to the Visual Studio Code editor features see the Microsoft Visual Studio Code User Guide."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Language Specific Features","url":"index.html#language-specific-features","text":"XSLT and XPath specific editing features are described in the Editing XSLT\/XPath page of the documentation that your are reading now. More general XML editing features are described in the Editing XML section. The features described at Editing XSLT\/XPath include: IntelliSense (Auto Complete) Syntax Highlighting Code Formatting Code Refactoring Symbol Renaming Hover Help and Signature Help XSLT\/XPath Linter"},{"page":"Overview","section":"Run XSLT - Quick Start","url":"index.html#run-xslt-quick-start","text":"The quickest way to run the stylesheet in the active editor is Quick Run: press ⌘⌥R (Ctrl+Alt+R on Windows\/Linux) or click the play button in the editor title bar. The first run creates an XSLT task for you, which later runs reuse. To step through the stylesheet instead, set a breakpoint and click the debug button in the editor title bar, or press F5 - see the XSLT Debugger. XSLT transforms are run using Visual Studio Code Tasks. Before configuring an XSLT task you should ensure that you open a Workspace folder (⌘O) from Visual Studio Code. The configuration is saved in a tasks.json file in this workspace folder. To create your first task, use the Run Build Task command (⇧⌘B). As there is no currently configured task, you should be prompted to configure one (if another extension inhibits this, run Configure Task instead). You can then, select either the Saxon or Saxon-JS task. A task will be added to the tasks.json task with default properties. To further configure the task, these properties can be changed or other properties added. Auto-completion and hints are provided to assist in the task configuration. Next time you press ⇧⌘B your task will be run in a Task tab in the Terminal window (toggled with ⌘J)."},{"page":"Overview","section":"More on running XSLT","url":"index.html#more-on-running-xslt","text":"XSLT Tasks are explained in full in the Running XSLT section. More general information can be found in the Visual Studio Code Tasks documentation."},{"page":"What's New","section":"","url":"new-release.html","text":"Highlights of the features added in release 2.0 and later: Step-through debugging - debug stylesheets with SaxonJ (any edition, including Saxon-HE): breakpoints with conditions, hit counts and logpoints, stepping through templates, functions and multi-line XPath expressions - including inline functions - and the variables, context item and output so far, as you go Full language support for XSLT 4.0 in Saxon 13 - type-aware auto-completion, checks, hover help and refactoring for the XSLT 4.0 and XPath 4.0 features Saxon 13 implements: new syntax and item types, keyword arguments and optional parameters, xsl:switch, and documentation notes with xsl:note Code Documentation (for Saxon PE\/EE versions 12.8+) - documentation within xsl:note elements provides hover assistance using tags based on xqdoc along with Markdown descriptions. Records and enums - auto-completion, checks, hover help and refactoring for XPath 4.0 record and enumeration types, from map constructors to xsl:switch cases JSON and JNodes - XPath 4.0 path expressions on JSON, with auto-completion, checks and hover help for steps on JNodes with a record type, and JNode paths shown by xdm:debug() Quick Run - run the active stylesheet with a shortcut or the toolbar button, without configuring a task first - with SaxonJ, SaxonJS or the new SaxonC support Inferred imports - the top-level stylesheet of a module is found from the workspace, without opening it first, and the new XSLT Imports Panel shows its tree of imports and includes XML Catalogs - resolve the href of xsl:import and xsl:include, e.g. the http: URI of a shared library, to a local file with an OASIS XML catalog - also passed to Saxon when a stylesheet is run Editing - signature help and richer hover help for functions and templates, more auto-completion, and the new Wrap with... refactoring Print debugging - one auto-completed xsl:message for all the in-scope variables, formatted and colored with xdm:debug()"},{"page":"Settings","section":"","url":"settings.html","text":"Visual Studio Code's settings let you tailor your working environment to fit requirements for specific languages or extensions, or to suit your own preferences. A general guide to settings is provided by the Visual Studio Code documentation: User and Workspace Settings. This page lists the XSLT\/XPath extension's own settings, and the editor settings of most interest for XSLT and XPath."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Creating User and Workspace Settings","url":"settings.html#creating","text":"From the Command Palette (⇧⌘P) you can either invoke Preferences: Open User Settings or Preferences: Open Workspace Settings. Editor settings can also be applied in a language-specific way: for this, use the command Preferences: Configure Language Specific Settings. Settings are either edited in the Settings form or in a JSON editor. Open the JSON editor by clicking the Open Settings (JSON) button at the top right of the Editor Group. This opens the corresponding settings.json file directly. User settings apply wherever you use VS Code, so they suit paths on your own computer, such as the Saxon jar. Workspace settings are saved in the project's .vscode\/settings.json and apply to everyone who opens the project, so they suit settings a team shares, such as an XML catalog or XSLT packages."},{"page":"Settings","section":"XSLT\/XPath Extension Settings","url":"settings.html#xslt-settings","text":"To list the extension's settings in the Settings editor, type XSLT in its search box. All of them are listed below, with their defaults. The sections after the tables describe the most important ones."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Running and debugging","url":"settings.html#settings-running","text":"Setting Default What it does XSLT.tasks.saxonJar - The path of your Saxon jar file, needed to run or debug stylesheets with SaxonJ - see The Saxon jar. XSLT.tasks.quickRunProcessor \"xslt\" The processor Quick Run uses: \"xslt\" (SaxonJ), \"xslt-js\" (SaxonJS) or \"xslt-c\" (SaxonC). Switching processor in Quick Run saves the choice here. XSLT.tasks.java.enabled true Offer SaxonJ (xslt) tasks - see Task types. XSLT.tasks.js.enabled true Offer SaxonJS (xslt-js) tasks. XSLT.tasks.saxonc.enabled true Offer SaxonC (xslt-c) tasks. XSLT.tasks.saxonCPath - The folder containing the SaxonC command-line executable (Transform or Transform.exe) - see SaxonC tasks. XSLT.tasks.saxonCLicenseFileLocation - The path of SaxonC's saxon-license.lic file, passed to SaxonC as --licenseFileLocation: - see Licensing. XSLT.tasks.saxonCLibraryPaths - More folders to search for SaxonC's native libraries. The lib folder beside the executable is always included - see Native library path. XSLT.tasks.htmlParserJar - The path of the nu.validator htmlparser jar, added to SaxonJ's classpath for XSLT 4.0 stylesheets, for Saxon's parse-html() function. XSLT.debug.openOutput false When debugging, open the output written so far beside the stylesheet, at the first pause where there is some - see Output while debugging."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Editing and checking","url":"settings.html#settings-editing","text":"Setting Default What it does XSLT.resources.catalog \"\" An OASIS XML catalog file, for resolving the href of xsl:import and xsl:include - also passed to Saxon - see XML catalog. XSLT.resources.xsltPackages - The files for xsl:use-package names - see XSLT packages. XSLT.resources.inferParentFromWorkspace true For a module that another stylesheet imports or includes, find that stylesheet from the workspace's files, so references to its declarations are resolved without opening it first - see Inferred imports. XSLT.resources.useSourceFile \"latest\" Completion of element and attribute names in XPath: \"latest\" suggests the names that can occur at that point in the path, from the XML context file; \"none\" suggests the names already used in the stylesheet - see IntelliSense for XPath locations. XSLT.validation.externalFunctionPrefixes [] Prefixes of functions the processor provides, such as Java extension functions - calls of functions with these prefixes aren't checked. The quick fix for a function that isn't found adds its prefix - see Java extension functions. XSLT.validation.xslt30ItemTypesAndNotes false Allow XSLT 4.0 named item types and xsl:note in XSLT 3.0 stylesheets, for Saxon PE and EE 12.8 or later - see below."},{"page":"Settings","section":"The Saxon jar","url":"settings.html#saxon-jar","text":"To run or debug stylesheets with SaxonJ - Saxon for Java - set XSLT.tasks.saxonJar to the full path of your Saxon jar file, usually as a user setting, since the path is specific to your computer. Any Saxon edition works, including Saxon-HE. The Debugger needs Saxon 12 or later, and a Java runtime must be on the PATH: \"XSLT.tasks.saxonJar\": \"\/Users\/me\/saxon\/SaxonHE12-9J\/saxon-he-12.9.jar\" On Windows, write each \\ in the path as \\\\, as JSON requires - e.g. \"C:\\\\saxon\\\\saxon-he-12.9.jar\". For Saxon-PE or Saxon-EE, keep the licence file, saxon-license.lic, in the same folder as the jar. XSLT tasks refer to the setting as ${config:XSLT.tasks.saxonJar}. For downloading Saxon and Java, see SaxonJ tasks."},{"page":"Settings","section":"XSLT packages","url":"settings.html#xslt-packages","text":"To allow XSLT package names to be resolved to file paths, package details should be added to the setting XSLT.resources.xsltPackages. Sample XSLT package settings: \"XSLT.resources.xsltPackages\": [ { \"name\": \"example.com.package1\", \"version\": \"2.0\", \"path\": \"included1.xsl\" }, { \"name\": \"example.com.package2\", \"version\": \"2.0\", \"path\": \"features\/included2.xsl\" } ]"},{"page":"Settings","section":"XML catalog","url":"settings.html#xml-catalog","text":"To resolve the href of xsl:import and xsl:include - and the URIs of fixed-namespaces - with an OASIS XML catalog, and pass the catalog to Saxon when a stylesheet is run, set XSLT.resources.catalog to the catalog file's path, relative to the workspace folder. See XML Catalogs. \"XSLT.resources.catalog\": \"catalog.xml\""},{"page":"Settings","section":"Task types","url":"settings.html#task-types","text":"The extension provides a task type for each Saxon processor - xslt for SaxonJ, xslt-js for SaxonJS and xslt-c for SaxonC - and all three are enabled by default. To stop a processor's tasks being offered, for example in Run Build Task, turn off its setting: \"XSLT.tasks.java.enabled\": true, \"XSLT.tasks.js.enabled\": true, \"XSLT.tasks.saxonc.enabled\": true See Tasks for configuring each task type."},{"page":"Settings","section":"XSLT 4.0 Item Types and Notes in XSLT 3.0","url":"settings.html#xslt30-item-types","text":"Saxon PE and EE 12.8 or later, run with syntax extensions, accept XSLT 4.0's named item types and xsl:note in a stylesheet with version=\"3.0\". To edit such stylesheets with the same support as for XSLT 4.0, enable the setting XSLT.validation.xslt30ItemTypesAndNotes - it's off by default. For a team, add it to the workspace settings, in the project's .vscode\/settings.json: \"XSLT.validation.xslt30ItemTypesAndNotes\": true With the setting, in an XSLT 3.0 stylesheet: xsl:item-type declarations are accepted, with record(...) and enum(...) types - which can also be used directly in as attributes the record and enumeration type support applies: completions of record fields, map keys and enumeration values, checks of map constructors, lookups and values, hover help, and renaming a record field xsl:note needs no use-when attribute - see Code Documentation Other XSLT 4.0 and XPath 4.0 features - such as xsl:switch, keyword arguments and fn(...) types - still need version=\"4.0\", and are reported as errors in an XSLT 3.0 stylesheet. Without the setting, xsl:item-type and record and enumeration types are reported as errors in an XSLT 3.0 stylesheet, and an xsl:note gets a warning unless it's excluded with use-when=\"false()\", as an XSLT 3.0 processor reports XTSE0010 for it. To run the stylesheets, Saxon needs syntax extensions: the --allowSyntaxExtensions:on option on the command line, or allowSyntaxExtensions=\"true\" in a Saxon configuration file. The extension's XSLT tasks and Quick Run enable it with the default \"allowSyntaxExtensions40\": \"auto\" task property, for Saxon PE and EE."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Converting Saxon type aliases","url":"settings.html#saxon-type-aliases","text":"Saxon's earlier syntax for named types - saxon:type-alias, tuple(...) types and ~name for a reference to a type alias - is ignored by Saxon 12.8 and later, which only report the references. The linter warns about a saxon:type-alias, and the command XSLT: Convert Saxon Type Aliases to xsl:item-type - also the warning's quick fix - converts them in all the workspace's files, shown for review in the refactor preview first: <saxon:type-alias name=\"dfx:bounds\" type=\"...\"\/> becomes <xsl:item-type name=\"dfx:bounds\" as=\"...\"\/> tuple(t: xs:integer, label?: xs:string) becomes record(t as xs:integer, label? as xs:string) as=\"~dfx:bounds\" becomes as=\"dfx:bounds\" A type alias with an extensible tuple type, e.g. tuple(a: xs:string, *), isn't converted - nor is one that references it - as XSLT 4.0 has no extensible record types. These are listed after the conversion, to convert by hand."},{"page":"Settings","section":"General Settings For XSLT","url":"settings.html#general-settings","text":""},{"page":"Settings","section":"Default Editor Settings for XSLT, XPath and XML","url":"settings.html#default-settings","text":"The extension sets these VS Code editor settings for the xslt, xpath and xml languages - for .xsl, .xslt, .xpath and XML files. As they're set for each language, they don't affect files in other languages. Any of them can be changed in a language-specific setting, e.g. \"[xslt]\": { \"editor.tabSize\": 4 } - see Creating User and Workspace Settings. Setting XSLT and XPath XML What it does editor.defaultFormatter \"deltaxml.xslt-xpath\" This extension formats the file - see Code Formatting. editor.formatOnType true Lines are indented as you type, e.g. a new line, or a closing tag. editor.formatOnPaste true Pasted text is indented to fit where it's pasted. editor.formatOnSave false The file isn't formatted when it's saved. editor.formatOnSaveMode \"file\" If formatOnSave is turned on, the whole file is formatted - or only the lines changed, with \"modifications\". editor.tabSize 2 4 The number of spaces for each level of indentation. editor.insertSpaces true Indentation uses spaces, not tabs. editor.detectIndentation true For an existing file, the tab size and spaces or tabs are detected from its content, instead of the two settings above. editor.wordSeparators \"`~!@#%^&*()=+[{]}\\\\|;'\\\",:<>\/?$\" Unlike VS Code's default, - and . aren't word separators, so double-clicking selects a whole name, such as current-group - see Word Separators. editor.semanticHighlighting.enabled true - Syntax highlighting for XSLT and XPath, which uses semantic tokens - see Syntax Highlighting. It's also set for the xsl language. outline.showArrays false The Outline view doesn't show arrays - see Code Navigation. The default settings, as JSON \"[xslt]\": { \"editor.defaultFormatter\": \"deltaxml.xslt-xpath\", \"editor.formatOnSaveMode\": \"file\", \"editor.formatOnSave\": false, \"editor.formatOnPaste\": true, \"editor.formatOnType\": true, \"editor.semanticHighlighting.enabled\": true, \"editor.detectIndentation\": true, \"editor.wordSeparators\": \"`~!@#%^&*()=+[{]}\\\\|;'\\\",:<>\/?$\", \"editor.insertSpaces\": true, \"editor.tabSize\": 2, \"outline.showArrays\": false }, \"[xpath]\": { \"editor.defaultFormatter\": \"deltaxml.xslt-xpath\", \"editor.formatOnSaveMode\": \"file\", \"editor.formatOnSave\": false, \"editor.formatOnPaste\": true, \"editor.formatOnType\": true, \"editor.semanticHighlighting.enabled\": true, \"editor.detectIndentation\": true, \"editor.wordSeparators\": \"`~!@#%^&*()=+[{]}\\\\|;'\\\",:<>\/?$\", \"editor.insertSpaces\": true, \"editor.tabSize\": 2, \"outline.showArrays\": false }, \"[xml]\": { \"editor.defaultFormatter\": \"deltaxml.xslt-xpath\", \"editor.formatOnSaveMode\": \"file\", \"editor.formatOnSave\": false, \"editor.formatOnPaste\": true, \"editor.formatOnType\": true, \"editor.wordSeparators\": \"`~!@#%^&*()=+[{]}\\\\|;'\\\",:<>\/?$\", \"editor.detectIndentation\": true, \"editor.insertSpaces\": true, \"editor.tabSize\": 4, \"outline.showArrays\": false }, \"[xsl]\": { \"editor.semanticHighlighting.enabled\": true }"},{"page":"Settings","section":"Built-in Editing Behaviour for XSLT","url":"settings.html#language-behaviour","text":"The extension also defines how the editor treats XSLT files in ways that aren't settings, so they can't be changed: Comments - Toggle Block Comment (⇧⌥A) wraps the selection in <!-- and -->. In .xpath files it uses XPath's (: and :). Auto-closing - typing {, [, (, a quote or a backtick adds the closing character. So does &, which adds ; for an entity reference, and (:, which adds : to start an XPath comment. Surrounding - typing one of these characters with text selected surrounds the selection with the pair, e.g. selecting $total and typing ( gives ($total). Words - a prefixed name such as xsl:template, a variable reference such as $total-price, and an attribute name test such as @select each count as one word - for example, when VS Code finds the word at the cursor for word-based suggestions. Folding regions - <?region?> and <?endregion?> processing instructions mark a region that can be folded - see Folding."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Code Formatting","url":"settings.html#code-formatting","text":"The following settings directly affect code formatting in the XSLT\/XPath extension: \"editor.defaultFormatter\": \"deltaxml.xslt-xpath\", \"editor.formatOnSaveMode\": \"modifications\", \"editor.formatOnSave\": true, \"editor.formatOnPaste\": true, \"editor.formatOnType\": true The editor.formatOnSaveMode setting can be used to only format XSLT lines changed since the last commit on Git."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Syntax Highlighting","url":"settings.html#syntax-highlighting","text":"Syntax highlighting for XSLT and XPath is achieved using Visual Studio Code's Semantic Highlighting tokens alone. Almost all other languages in Visual Studio Code use a TextMate Grammar for tokens, which may be supplemented by Semantic Highlighting. If XSLT and XPath are not syntax highlighted in a Color Theme, you can explicitly enable Semantic Highlighting with the setting: \"editor.semanticHighlighting.enabled\": true The XSLT Dark Themes extension provides a selection of popular dark color themes, enhanced for XSLT\/XPath. Screenshot of Gruvbox Theme extended for XSLT X Gruvbox Material Dark theme"},{"page":"Settings","section":"Word Separators","url":"settings.html#word-separators","text":"The editor.wordSeparators setting affects how the full 'word' is selected when you double-click on a part of the word. The characters listed are used as word separators. Typically in XSLT, you do not want characters like - and . treated as word separators, as these characters are valid within a QName like an attribute name or a variable name."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Emmet Snippets","url":"settings.html#emmet","text":"Emmet Snippets provide a convenient shorthand for inserting a number of literal result elements and attributes into your XSLT in one go. For example, entering: ul>li.item$*2 provides the following output: <ul> <li class=\"item1\"><\/li> <li class=\"item2\"><\/li> <\/ul> To enable Emmet snippets for XSLT, add the following setting: \"emmet.includeLanguages\": { \"xslt\": \"xml\" }"},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"","url":"editing.html","text":"XSLT developers benefit from a set of XML editing features that are also available for any XML file. These general purpose features are described here."},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"XML Elements","url":"editing.html#xml-elements","text":""},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"Rename Tag","url":"editing.html#rename-tag","text":"As you edit the start tag name of an element, the corresponding end tag name is changed at the same time to keep the XML well-nested."},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"Self-Close Element","url":"editing.html#self-close-element","text":"To convert an element with a start-tag and end-tag to a self-closed element: With the cursor immediately to the left of the >at the end of the start-tag, type \/. Any element contents and the end tag will be deleted automatically: <d><para\/>test<\/para<\/d> --> <d><para\/><\/d>"},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"Rename all elements with selected name","url":"editing.html#rename-all-elements-with-selected-name","text":"Select the element tag name you want to rename by double-clicking it, then press (⇧⌘L). This selects all matching tag names so you're in a multi-cursor edit mode. As you edit the tag name you selected all matching tag names are edited also. When done, press Escape to exit multi-cursor edit."},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"Auto-Completion","url":"editing.html#auto-completion","text":"Outside of the auto-complete (intellisense) features for XSLT and XPath, a small set of snippets for general purpose XML elements is accessible from the auto-completion list: With the cursor in the element start tag where you wish to add the attribute, press ⌃Space and select attribute to insert the code snippet: name=\"value\" Edit the name and then press Tab to edit the value."},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"Emmet","url":"editing.html#emmet","text":"Emmet can be used to insert XML snippets using a shorthand notation. Emmet should be enabled in user settings. See the Settings section for an example."},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"Well-Formed XML Checks","url":"editing.html#well-formed-xml-checks","text":"As you edit an XML file, the syntax is checked for any well-formedness errors. Any problems tokens are highlighted with a squiggly underline in the editor. To see a description of the problem, hover over a highlighted token. To help navigate between problems, the Problems View (press ⇧⌘M to toggle) shows a list of XML syntax problems. Problems are also highlighted in the Outline View. Selecting a Problem View list item, or Outline View node will navigate you to the corresponding token in the XML editor."},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"XML Formatting","url":"editing.html#xmlformatting","text":"When you have an XML file open, normal XML formatting features are available. With typical hand-edited XML, it is only indentation that is correct by the formatting. However, if no newline characters are found at the start of the XML file, it is assumed the contents is system generated and newline characters are added for elements that are not mixed content. The formatter observes the normal rules for whitespace preservation in XML. So xml:space attributes affect whether indentation is preserved. Whitespace in CDATA sections and 'significant' whitespace in content inferred to be mixed content is also preserved. XML attributes on newlines and multi-line attribute value are indented by the XML formatting provider to reflect the level of nesting."},{"page":"Editing XML","section":"XML Element Selection","url":"editing.html#xml-element-selection","text":"The XSLT\/XPath extension provides a set of commands for selecting an element. These commands can be invoked from the Command Palette (⇧⌘P), or from the XML Selection submenu of the editor's context menu (right-click), which also shows their keyboard shortcuts. From the keyboard, XML: Select Element... (⇧⌘2) shows the same commands in a quick pick. On Windows and Linux, the shortcuts use Ctrl+Shift instead of ⇧⌘, for example Ctrl+Shift+0. Goto XPath - navigate to the element\/attribute given by the XPath Select current element - ⇧⌘0 extend selection to include containing element's start\/end tags Select parent element - ⇧⌘9 extend selection to include parent element's start\/end tags Select first child element - ⇧⌘8 select the first child element's start\/end tags Select following element - ⇧⌘7 select next sibling's start\/end tags Select preceding element - ⇧⌘6 select previous sibling's start\/end tags"},{"page":"Editing XSLT\/XPath","section":"","url":"editing-xslt.html","text":"With DeltaXignia's XSLT\/XPath extension , Visual Studio Code is now a fully featured XSLT 3.0 editor. Standalone XPath 3.1 files are also fully supported. Visual Studio Code's rich ecosystem is now available to XSLT and XPath developers. Comprehensive, but language-neutral, descriptions of Visual Studio Code can be found in Microsoft's Visual Studio Code User Guide. Links to key sections are below: Keyboard shortcuts Multiple selections (multi-cursor) Find and Replace File encoding support Emmet (snippet abbreviations) The documentation here focuses on XSLT and XPath language features. More general XML features are covered on the Editing XML page. Help on navigating code in the editor is provided in the Code Navigation page."},{"page":"Editing XSLT\/XPath","section":"Syntax Highlighting","url":"editing-xslt.html#highlighting","text":"Eva Dark Theme For improved performance and precision, this extension avoids using the default but problematic TM Grammar. Instead we use Visual Studio Code's Semantic Highlighting introduced in 2020. For color themes extensions you should explicitly enable Semantic Highlighting. See the Settings page for more information. Github Light Theme The extension provides syntax highlighting for XSLT instructions, XML Literal Result Elements, XML attributes, XML character references, CDATA sections and all tokens within XPath expressions. XPath expressions within Attribute Value Templates (AVTs) and Text Value Templates are syntax highlighted also."},{"page":"Editing XSLT\/XPath","section":"XSLT\/XPath Dark Color Themes","url":"editing-xslt.html#xslt-xpath-dark-color-themes","text":"The plethora of VS Code themes will highlight XSLT\/XPath very well using Semantic Highlighting fallbacks for language-specific tokens. There is however a XSLT Dark Themes extension that enhances a set of popular dark themes specifically for XSLT and XPath: Darcula From IntelliJ Gruvbox Material Dark Iceberg Nord Tokyo Night Storm Zen"},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"","url":"navigation.html","text":"Visual Studio Code includes a compelling set of features for code navigation, the language-neutral details are outlined here. This page outlines code navigation from an XSLT and XPath development perspective."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"File Navigation","url":"navigation.html#file-navigation","text":"While the Explorer View is used for workspace browsing, pressing ⌘P, for the Quick Open command, provides a quick-pick list of available files that is filtered\/highlighted as you type. File tabs are arranged in one or more Editor Groups. Editor Groups are arranged either adjacent or above\/below other Editor groups. The same file may be open in more than one Editor Group. There are settings to control whether file tabs are scrollable or wrapped within an Editor Group. Hold Ctrl and press Tab for a list of files in an Editor Group. Keep pressing Tab until you reach the file you want and then release Ctrl to open it."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Previous\/Next Location","url":"navigation.html#previous-next-location","text":"Many actions can be used to navigate you to a new location. Press ⌃- for Previous Location and ⌃⇧- for Next Location. Locations may be in the same file or different files."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"File Links","url":"navigation.html#file-links","text":"In XSLT, The href attribute values of xsl:import, xsl:include and xsl:use-package have a solid underline if the file path is resolved. Press ⌘+Click on the file path to navigate to that file."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"XSLT\/XML Breadcrumbs","url":"navigation.html#xslt-xml-breadcrumbs","text":"The Breadcrumb View, shown at the top of the editor, shows the current element or attribute location within an XML or XSLT file with respect to ancestor elements. In XSLT Breadcrumbs, an identifier is often shown as well as the element name. This may be for example the name of an xsl:variable or the mode of an xsl:template. The symbol names are the same as those shown in the Outline view. Clicking on a Breadcrumb item will show a dropdown Outline view which you can then navigate through with the mouse, touch or cursor keys."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Keyboard Navigation","url":"navigation.html#keyboard-navigation","text":"Press ⇧⌘. (note the 'dot' character) to select the last Breadcrumb item and open with a dropdown outline. You can then use cursor keys to navigate the dropdown outline. Alternatively, press ⇧⌘; (note the 'semi-colon' character) to select the last Breadcrumb item without a dropdown outline. Use the ← and → keys to select a Breadcrumb item or the ↓ for the dropdown outline. Note: You can toggle the Breadcrumbs view on\/off but if the Breadcrumbs view is disabled this will also disable XML, XSLT or XPath Warnings and Errors. The Code Checker depends on the symbol name provider that Breadcrumbs invokes."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Outline View","url":"navigation.html#outline-view","text":"The Outline View (see right-hand panel) is a panel that, by default, is positioned below the File Explorer in the Primary Side Bar. This can be moved however into other panels such as the Secondary Side Bar (see Sidebars). The Outline view renders the XML or XSLT document element hierarchy as a tree, symbol names in the tree include element identifiers such as the mode for an xsl:template. Symbols in the Outline View can be sorted by position, name or category. There's also a 'Filter on Type' mode that filters the Outline View while you type in part of a symbol name. Note: XML Attributes are shown in the outline view by default, they are grouped inside an element within an attributes 'array' symbol. If you find attributes clutter the XML element outline you can hide them in Settings with the property: outline.showArrays."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Peek\/Goto Definition","url":"navigation.html#peek-goto-definition","text":"A symbol, such as a variable or function, is defined in one location in a stylesheet file, but used in other locations. The Peek and Goto commands let you either view inline or navigate to the definition of a selected symbol."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Peek Definition","url":"navigation.html#peek-definition","text":"Invoke the Peek Definition command by pressing (⌥F12) on any symbol name such as a variable or function name. This command embeds a view of the definition inline avoiding the context-switch arising from the Goto Definition command. The inline definition view permits quick edits of the definition also. Press Escape to close the definition view."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Goto Definition","url":"navigation.html#goto-definition","text":"Invoke the Goto Definition command by pressing (F12) on any symbol name such as a variable or function name. This command navigates you to the location of the symbol definition (for example, the corresponding xsl:variable instruction), opening the containing stylesheet file in the editor group if not already open. start"},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Goto (Peek) References\/Find All References","url":"navigation.html#goto-peek-references-find-all-references","text":"This feature is about navigating to references to symbols, in contrast to the previous section that covers navigating to symbol definitions. The Peek and Find All commands let you either view inline or navigate through all the references (including the definition) of a selected symbol."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Goto (Peek) References","url":"navigation.html#goto-peek-references","text":"Invoke the Goto (Peek) References command by pressing (⇧F12) on any symbol name such as a variable or function name. This command embeds a view of the resolved references inline, use the cursor keys to navigate between each reference listed. The inline view permits quick edits of the reference also. Press Escape to close the references view."},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Find All References","url":"navigation.html#find-all-references","text":"Invoke the Find All References command by pressing (⌥⇧F12) on any symbol. A References Panel, shown in the application Side Bar, lists all references, grouped by their stylesheet module. Use the cursor keys to move through the references and press Enter to open the editor in the reference location. After navigating to a reference or definition, you can quickly navigate to the location you were in using Ctrl- end"},{"page":"Code Navigation","section":"Goto Symbol","url":"navigation.html#goto-symbol","text":"Use the Goto Symbol in Editor command Shift⌘O for a filtered quick-pick list of symbols used in the current editor. The text you enter filters the list, the ↑ and ↓ keys navigate you through the symbol list, with the current symbol item highlighted in the editor. Press Enter to navigate to the symbol in the current editor or ⌘Enter to open an adjacent editor and navigate to the symbol there."},{"page":"Refactoring","section":"","url":"refactoring-xslt.html","text":"The Refactoring section of the Editing XSLT\/XPath part of this guide describes XSLT refactoring in general. This section provides additional detail on the 'extract to xsl:function refactor.."},{"page":"Refactoring","section":"Introduction","url":"refactoring-xslt.html#intro","text":"There are two available modes for extracting selected code to an xsl:function: full and partial. In this refactor, required context properties must be passed as parameters when the new xsl:function is invoked, this is because context properties are cleared inside an xsl:function."},{"page":"Refactoring","section":"Partial Mode","url":"refactoring-xslt.html#partial-mode","text":"In the partial mode, required parameters are passed to the new function, but code in not refactored to reference the new parameters. Instead, the 'missing-context' properties are highlighted as problems."},{"page":"Refactoring","section":"Full Mode","url":"refactoring-xslt.html#full-mode","text":"The full refactor fixes 'missing-context' properties by refactoring the code to reference the relevant parameters."},{"page":"Refactoring","section":"A Sample Refactor","url":"refactoring-xslt.html#example","text":"The screenshots below show sample code refactored to extract to an xsl:function. 1. The original code selected for refactoring 2. After a partial refactor: 'missing-context' problems are highlighted 2. After a full refactor: there are no 'missing-context' problems as code is updated in the refactor to let parameters provide the context"},{"page":"Refactoring","section":"Known Restrictions","url":"refactoring-xslt.html#restrictions","text":"The code selected for a refactor is important. For example, in many cases it may be more effective to extract an entire xsl:merge instruction instead of a set of instructions within the child xsl:merge-action instruction. There are known limitations in what can be achieved when passing context to an xsl:function, these are outlined below. Please note also that in general there will be cases where, because of context, it would be advisable to extract to xsl:template instead of xsl:function. Potential ‘missing context’ problems for the xsl:call-template instruction are highlighted but not fixed. In such cases, it’s advisable to undo the refactor and use the extract to xsl:template refactoring instead. The regex-group($n) expression (where $n is not a literal integer) with ‘missing context’ is a special refactoring case. It is resolved to $r.group($n) where $r.group references a context xsl:param of type map(xs:integer, xs:string), The number of items in the generated code for $r.group is set to 99 this number should be corrected manually to the expected number of regex groups given the regex used."},{"page":"Refactoring","section":"Context Properties","url":"refactoring-xslt.html#context-properties","text":"The table below lists the context properties, along with the corresponding xsl:function parameter names that will, if required, be created for an xsl:function refactor. Context property Parameter current() or '.' c.x position() c.p last() c.l current-group() g.current current-grouping-key() g.key regex-group($n) r.group($n) current-merge-group() m.current current-merge-key() m.key current-merge-group() g.current current-merge-group($name) g.groups($name)"},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"","url":"xml-catalogs.html","text":"An XML catalog maps the URIs used in a stylesheet to the files they're resolved to. With a catalog, a stylesheet can import a shared library by a fixed name, such as http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/strings.xsl, wherever the library's files are kept - only the catalog changes when they move. Catalogs are an OASIS standard, XML Catalogs 1.1, and Saxon supports them with its -catalog option. The XSLT\/XPath extension uses a catalog to resolve the href of xsl:import and xsl:include - so the declarations of a module imported by its catalog name are known to the linter, auto-completion, hover help and go to definition - and passes the same catalog to Saxon when a stylesheet is run. Catalogs are useful when: a team shares XSLT libraries across projects, and each project keeps its copy of a library in a different place stylesheets import libraries by http: URIs, and the files should be read locally - for speed, or to work offline libraries are reorganised, or a new version is tried, without editing the stylesheets that import them To try it out, see the catalog demo."},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"Setting up a catalog","url":"xml-catalogs.html#setup","text":"Set XSLT.resources.catalog to the path of the catalog file - relative to the workspace folder, or with ${workspaceFolder}. For a team, add it to the workspace settings, in the project's .vscode\/settings.json: \"XSLT.resources.catalog\": \"catalog.xml\" The setting names one master catalog. It can hand lookups on to any number of other catalogs, with nextCatalog entries - for example, a catalog for each library. When there's no setting, and the root of the workspace folder has a catalog.xml that's an XML catalog, you're asked whether to use it: Use adds the setting to the workspace settings, and Don't Ask Again is remembered for the workspace. To use no catalog, and not be asked, set the setting to \"\". Changes to the setting, and to the catalog files, are picked up straight away."},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"Catalog files","url":"xml-catalogs.html#catalog-files","text":"A catalog is an XML file whose root element is catalog, in the namespace urn:oasis:names:tc:entity:xmlns:xml:catalog. Each entry maps a URI - the name used in an href - to the URI of a file. A relative path in a catalog is relative to the catalog file itself. <catalog xmlns=\"urn:oasis:names:tc:entity:xmlns:xml:catalog\"> <uri name=\"http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/strings.xsl\" uri=\"lib\/strings\/strings.xsl\"\/> <\/catalog> With this catalog, <xsl:import href=\"http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/strings.xsl\"\/> imports lib\/strings\/strings.xsl. The name needn't be a real web address: it's an identifier for the library. The extension uses these entries: uri - maps one URI, its name, to the URI of a file, its uri rewriteURI - maps every URI that starts with its uriStartString, replacing that part with its rewritePrefix, and keeping the rest - e.g. a whole folder of libraries: <rewriteURI uriStartString=\"http:\/\/example.com\/lib\/\" rewritePrefix=\"lib\/\"\/> maps http:\/\/example.com\/lib\/strings\/strings.xsl to lib\/strings\/strings.xsl uriSuffix - maps every URI that ends with its uriSuffix to the URI of a file nextCatalog - another catalog file, its catalog, to try when this catalog has no entry for a URI group - groups entries, e.g. to give them an xml:base - which can also be on the catalog element or an entry, to set the base URI that its relative paths are resolved against In each catalog, an exact uri entry is used first, then the rewriteURI entry with the longest uriStartString that matches, then the uriSuffix entry with the longest uriSuffix - and only if none matches, the nextCatalog entries, in order. An href is looked up as it's written, and then, if it's relative, as the absolute URI it resolves to."},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"In the editor","url":"xml-catalogs.html#editing","text":"For an xsl:import or xsl:include whose href the catalog resolves: the module's declarations are known, as for a module imported by its file path Cmd\/Ctrl+click on the href opens the module's file hover help on the href shows how the catalog resolved it: the entry that matched, and the catalog files it was found through - e.g. Resolved by the XML catalog: the uri entry for http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/strings.xsl in catalogs\/libraries.xml, via catalog.xml Hover on a catalog-resolved href, showing the entry and the catalog chain. An href that's a URI the catalog doesn't resolve to a file is reported, naming the catalog: an http: or https: URI is a warning - its declarations aren't known, but Saxon will try to fetch it when the stylesheet is run another URI, e.g. urn:example:strings, is an error - Saxon can't resolve it either In the Problems panel, the problem has a link to the catalog - and Cmd\/Ctrl+click on the href opens the catalog, to add an entry for it. With no catalog, the problem suggests using one. The warning for an http: href that isn't in the catalog."},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"Catalog files in the editor","url":"xml-catalogs.html#catalog-files-in-the-editor","text":"In a catalog file - one that the catalog of the setting reads, or any XML file with a catalog root element in the catalog namespace: the uri of each entry, and the catalog of each nextCatalog entry, is a document link, so you can Cmd\/Ctrl+click from the master catalog, through the catalogs, to a library file an entry whose file - or, for a rewritePrefix, folder - isn't found has a warning, e.g. after a library has moved A catalog file, with a warning for an entry whose file isn't found"},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"Running stylesheets with the catalog","url":"xml-catalogs.html#running","text":"The XSLT tasks for SaxonJ (xslt) and SaxonC (xslt-c) - including those Quick Run creates - pass the catalog of the setting to Saxon, with the -catalog option, so the stylesheet is run with the same imports as it's edited with. A task's own catalogFilenames property is used instead, when it has one: \"catalogFilenames\": \"${workspaceFolder}\/other-catalog.xml\" Saxon uses the catalog for all the URIs it resolves - including doc(), document() and unparsed-text(), and DTDs - not only imports. So the catalog is always passed, even when the stylesheet's imports don't need it."},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"Limitations","url":"xml-catalogs.html#limitations","text":"The setting names one master catalog - use nextCatalog entries for others. The editor uses the uri, rewriteURI, uriSuffix and nextCatalog entries - not system, public or delegate entries, which are for DTDs and other external entities. Saxon uses them all. Saxon-JS tasks aren't passed the catalog."},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"Catalog demo","url":"xml-catalogs.html#demo","text":"The catalog-demo folder is a small, complete example: a master catalog that hands lookups on to a secondary catalog, which maps the URIs of two XSLT libraries, in different folders, to their files - and a top-level stylesheet that imports both libraries by those URIs. Clone the repository, or copy the files, to try it. catalog-demo\/ ├── catalog.xml master catalog: a nextCatalog entry for catalogs\/libraries.xml ├── catalogs\/ │ └── libraries.xml secondary catalog: a uri entry for each library ├── lib\/ │ ├── strings\/strings.xsl imported as http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/strings.xsl │ └── dates\/dates.xsl imported as http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/dates.xsl ├── main.xsl imports both libraries by their catalog names └── .vscode\/settings.json \"XSLT.resources.catalog\": \"catalog.xml\" How http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/strings.xsl is resolved: catalog.xml has no entry for it, so its nextCatalog entry is tried: catalogs\/libraries.xml libraries.xml has a uri entry with that name, whose uri is ..\/lib\/strings\/strings.xsl - relative to libraries.xml so the import is lib\/strings\/strings.xsl"},{"page":"XML Catalogs","section":"Try it","url":"xml-catalogs.html#try-it","text":"Open the catalog-demo folder in VS Code (File > Open Folder...), so that its .vscode\/settings.json applies Open main.xsl: there are no problems, and hover help on str:shout shows the function's signature, from strings.xsl Hover on an href, to see the entry that resolved it - and Cmd\/Ctrl+click it, to open the library file Open catalog.xml, and follow its links to catalogs\/libraries.xml and the library files Run main.xsl with Quick Run: the result is e.g. CATALOGS WORK! | Wednesday 30 September 2026 Then: Change an href to a URI that isn't in the catalog, e.g. http:\/\/example.com\/xslt\/missing.xsl, to see the warning - and Cmd\/Ctrl+click it to open the catalog Move lib\/dates to another folder: its entry in catalogs\/libraries.xml has a warning. Update the entry's uri - main.xsl doesn't change Replace the two uri entries with the rewriteURI entry in the comment in libraries.xml, and change the hrefs in main.xsl to match Delete .vscode\/settings.json and reopen the folder, to be offered catalog.xml From the command line, the same catalog is passed to Saxon with -catalog: java -cp saxon-he-13.0.jar net.sf.saxon.Transform -xsl:main.xsl -it -catalog:catalog.xml"},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"","url":"quick-run.html","text":"Quick Run is the fastest way to run an XSLT stylesheet, without first creating a task by hand. It can run the stylesheet with any of the three Saxon processors: SaxonJ (Java), SaxonJS (JavaScript) or SaxonC (native C\/C++). It reuses the same command-line arguments the corresponding XSLT task would use, but takes the stylesheet and XML source directly from the editor rather than requiring a pre-configured tasks.json entry. (1) XSLT in active editor, (2) the Saxon processor, (3) the XML context file - Click the play button to run"},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"Running a stylesheet","url":"quick-run.html#running-a-stylesheet","text":"With an XSLT file open in the active editor, invoke Quick Run in one of three ways: Press ⌘⌥R (Ctrl+Alt+R on Windows\/Linux) Click the play button in the editor title bar - its tooltip names the processor currently in use, for example Quick Run with SaxonC Run Quick Run with Saxon from the Command Palette The stylesheet is the active editor's file. The XML source is the current XML context file - the same file used to provide XML node-names when auto-completing XPath location steps, shown in the status bar. This means the context file now serves two purposes: XPath auto-completion, and as the Quick Run source document. An XML context file must be set before Quick Run can be used - unless the stylesheet declares an xsl:initial-template, which Quick Run then starts from (see Starting from xsl:initial-template below). Each processor also needs its own setup: SaxonJ - the XSLT.tasks.saxonJar setting must point at your Saxon jar file. See the Saxon (Java) setup section on the XSLT Tasks page. SaxonJS - requires a NodeJS installation; the xslt3 package is run using NPX. See the Saxon-JS Tasks section on the XSLT Tasks page. SaxonC - the XSLT.tasks.saxonCPath setting must point at the folder containing the SaxonC Transform executable (Transform.exe on Windows). See the SaxonC Tasks section on the XSLT Tasks page for setup details, including native library paths and licensing. Tasks for each processor can be turned off with the XSLT.tasks.java.enabled, XSLT.tasks.js.enabled and XSLT.tasks.saxonc.enabled settings. Quick Run reports an error if the selected processor's tasks are disabled."},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"Running a module that another stylesheet imports","url":"quick-run.html#modules","text":"A module that another stylesheet imports or includes may use functions, variables or templates declared in other modules of that stylesheet. In the editor, these references are resolved through the top-level stylesheet - see Inferred Imports - but the module can't be compiled, and so can't be run, on its own. When you Quick Run such a module, it's checked first - with the modules it imports and includes - and if it depends on the top-level stylesheet, you're offered the choice: Run top-level stylesheet - runs the top-level stylesheet instead, with the same XML context file and processor Run Anyway - runs the module itself The choice is remembered for the module until Visual Studio Code is restarted. A module that can be run on its own is run straight away, with no prompt. The top-level stylesheet can also be run from the ▶ button in the XSLT Imports view, whichever of its modules is being edited."},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"Choosing the processor: SaxonJ, SaxonJS or SaxonC","url":"quick-run.html#choosing-the-processor-saxonj-saxonjs-or-saxonc","text":"Quick Run uses SaxonJ by default. When an XSLT or XML file is active, the processor currently selected is shown in the status bar, for example ▶ SaxonJ. To switch processor, use any of the following: Click the processor name in the status bar Hold ⌥ (Alt on Windows\/Linux) and click the play button in the editor title bar - while the key is held, the play button changes to a 'switch' button Run XSLT: Quick Run: Switch Processor from the Command Palette Each of these shows a list of the three processors to pick from, with the current one marked. The play button's tooltip and the status bar update straight away. The choice is stored in the XSLT.tasks.quickRunProcessor setting, whose values are the task types: xslt (SaxonJ), xslt-js (SaxonJS) and xslt-c (SaxonC). You can also edit this setting directly, either as a User setting or as a Workspace setting - for example, to always use SaxonC for one project. Switching processor updates the setting where it is already defined (the workspace folder, then the workspace), otherwise it updates your User settings. SaxonC, a native executable, starts much faster than SaxonJ, which needs a few hundred milliseconds to start the Java VM and load Saxon on each run. So SaxonC usually gives the quickest turnaround when running a stylesheet on small or medium inputs. For longer transforms, SaxonJ's Java JIT compiler makes it faster: in our tests, SaxonJ was ahead once execution took more than about two seconds."},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"First use: a task is created for you","url":"quick-run.html#first-use-a-task-is-created-for-you","text":"The first time Quick Run is used for a given stylesheet\/context-file pairing, a real task is added to your workspace's tasks.json file (creating the file if it doesn't exist yet) - not just an ephemeral, one-off run. The generated task: is a normal task for the selected processor: an xslt task for SaxonJ, an xslt-js task for SaxonJS or an xslt-c task for SaxonC is labelled from the stylesheet and context file names, for example demo with books.xml. SaxonJS and SaxonC task labels have the processor name added, for example demo with books.xml (SaxonC) uses the xslt-xpath.pickResultFile command for its result path, so you're prompted to choose (or reuse a recently used) output location - the same 'recent files' behaviour described in the Variable References in Tasks section of the XSLT Tasks page refers to the processor's setting (for example ${config:XSLT.tasks.saxonCPath}) rather than copying its current value, so the task keeps working if you later change the setting includes an entry in its parameters array for each stylesheet parameter that must be set - including those declared in imported or included stylesheets. Parameters with a default are left out, so the stylesheet's own defaults apply"},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"Required parameters","url":"quick-run.html#required-parameters","text":"A stylesheet parameter must be set when it has required=\"yes\", or when it has no default (no select attribute or content) and an as type that doesn't allow an empty sequence - for example as=\"xs:string\" or as=\"element()+\", but not as=\"xs:string?\". A parameter without an as attribute defaults to a zero-length string, so it isn't added. The value of each one comes from an input that's added to the inputs of tasks.json, so you're asked for it each time the task runs: \"parameters\": [ { \"name\": \"title\", \"value\": \"${input:xsltParam-title}\" }, { \"name\": \"?config\", \"value\": \"${input:xsltParam-config}\" } ] ... \"inputs\": [ { \"id\": \"xsltParam-title\", \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"xslt-xpath.enterParameterValue\", \"args\": { \"name\": \"title\", \"type\": \"xs:string\", \"xpath\": false } }, ... ] You can type a value, or pick one you've used before for a parameter of that name - the last one used is first, so Enter uses it again. Press Escape to cancel the run. Once you've settled on a value, you can replace the ${input:...} reference with the value itself. Parameters with the same name, in different tasks, share an input. For an atomic type, such as xs:string, xs:integer or xs:anyURI, the value is typed as it is, without quotes - Saxon converts it to the declared type. For other types, such as maps, arrays and nodes, the parameter name is prefixed with ?, for example ?config, which tells Saxon to evaluate the value as an XPath expression: map{'a': 1}, doc('data.xml'), or 'text' for a string."},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"Subsequent runs","url":"quick-run.html#subsequent-runs","text":"Once a task exists for a stylesheet\/context-file pairing, Quick Run re-runs that same task rather than creating another one. This means you can freely edit the generated task afterwards - change a parameter value, adjust the result path, add Saxon features or additional parameters - and Quick Run will keep using your edits on every subsequent run, just like running any other task. For different sets of parameters, copy the task in tasks.json and give the copy a new label. When more than one task is for the stylesheet\/context-file pairing, Quick Run (and debugging) asks which task to run - the tasks you've used most recently are listed first. Tasks are kept separately for each processor. If you switch processor, the next Quick Run for the same stylesheet\/context-file pairing creates a new task for that processor; your existing tasks for the other processors are left unchanged, and are used again if you switch back. If you want a fresh task instead, either edit the existing one directly, or remove it from tasks.json and Quick Run will create a new one next time it's used for that stylesheet\/context-file pairing. An alternative way to run a task created with Quick Run is to invoke the Tasks: Run Task command from the Command Palette, this will list all your tasks in a pick list. The tasks are sorted with the most recently run tasks at the top."},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"Starting from xsl:initial-template","url":"quick-run.html#initial-template","text":"A stylesheet that declares an xsl:initial-template can be run with no source document at all. When no XML context file has been set yet, Quick Run does this automatically. To keep running without a source document once XML files are opened - which otherwise become the context file - click the XML context file in the status bar and choose None, the last entry in the list of files. The status bar then shows [no XML context]. With no XML context file, Quick Run starts the stylesheet from xsl:initial-template (Saxon's -it command-line option) instead of applying templates to a source document. The xsl:initial-template can be declared in the stylesheet itself or in any module it imports or includes. If there isn't one, Quick Run reports an error and offers to pick an XML context file instead. The task created for this has an empty xmlSource and an empty initialTemplate property, and is labelled with the stylesheet name, for example report with xsl:initial-template. It is kept separately from any task for the same stylesheet that has an XML source, so you can switch between the two just by changing the context file. None stays selected until you pick a file again - opening or viewing other XML files does not replace it. While it is selected, XPath auto-completion has no source document to take element and attribute names from. A stylesheet can declare xsl:initial-template and also contain match templates designed for a source document, so Quick Run doesn't guess which way to run it: while an XML context file is set, it always applies templates to that file. The first time it creates a task for such a stylesheet, a notification points out the alternatives - add \"initialTemplate\": \"\" to the task to start from xsl:initial-template with the context file as the global context item, or choose None to run with no source document at all."},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"Running from an XML file","url":"quick-run.html#running-from-an-xml-file","text":"Quick Run can also be started from an XML file, to run a stylesheet on it without opening the stylesheet first. With an XML file open in the active editor, invoke it in one of three ways: Press ⌘⌥R (Ctrl+Alt+R on Windows\/Linux) Click the play button in the editor title bar Run XSLT: Quick Run: Run XSLT on this XML File from the Command Palette This shows a list of the XSLT tasks that can run on the file, grouped as: tasks for books.xml (named after the XML file) - every task whose xmlSource is this file, for any processor. This includes each task Quick Run has created for a stylesheet with this file as its source, so any stylesheet you've already run on the file is one click away. tasks for the current file - tasks whose xmlSource is ${file}, which Visual Studio Code replaces with whichever file is active when the task runs. Use this for general-purpose stylesheets you want to run on any XML file. Pick Stylesheet... - choose a stylesheet from the recently used list, from any <?xml-stylesheet?> processing instruction in the XML file, or from the file explorer. Quick Run then creates and runs a task for that stylesheet and this file, using the selected processor, exactly as it would from the stylesheet itself - so it appears under tasks for books.xml next time. Each task is listed with its processor and stylesheet path, and you can type to filter on any of these. If there are no matching tasks yet, Quick Run goes straight to picking a stylesheet. As with the stylesheet's play button, holding ⌥ (Alt on Windows\/Linux) while clicking the XML file's play button switches the processor instead. A task that runs on the current file looks like this: { \"type\": \"xslt\", \"label\": \"Summarise\", \"saxonJar\": \"${config:XSLT.tasks.saxonJar}\", \"xsltFile\": \"${workspaceFolder}\/tools\/summary.xsl\", \"xmlSource\": \"${file}\", \"resultPath\": \"${command:xslt-xpath.pickResultFile}\" }"},{"page":"Quick Run","section":"The XML context file is remembered","url":"quick-run.html#the-xml-context-file-is-remembered","text":"Your chosen XML context file is now restored automatically when Visual Studio Code is reopened, rather than defaulting back to the last XML file you happened to view. It's stored per workspace, separately from the 'recently used' file list, so viewing other XML files - for example a transform's result - does not change what gets restored. Choosing None is remembered in the same way. If the context file has since been deleted or moved, Quick Run - and debugging - say so instead of running, with a Pick XML Context File button to choose another. For custom parameters and features beyond what's auto-populated, chaining tasks together, or any other task configuration in detail, see the XSLT Tasks page - a task Quick Run creates is a normal task, so everything described there still applies to it."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"","url":"run-xslt.html","text":"Visual Studio Code's Tasks include custom task types for configuring and running Saxon (Java) or Saxon-JS (JavaScript) XSLT transforms. The Saxon XSLT processor is called via its command-line interface from the Integrated Terminal in Visual Studio code. Task configurations effectively control command-line arguments for the XSLT Processor. An attempt is made to parse any error messages returned from Saxon, so that the position of any Saxon-reported errors in the XSLT can be highlighted. XSLT transforms are run using custom xslt or xslt-js Visual Studio Code Tasks for Saxon (requires Java and Saxon installation) or Saxon-JS (requires NodeJS installation) respectively."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Saxon (Java) setup","url":"run-xslt.html#saxonj-tasks","text":"Ensure that you have a workspace folder open in Visual Studio Code (use File > open... from the menu). The configuration will be saved in a tasks.json file in this workspace folder. If Java is not already installed, Java 1.8 or later can be downloaded and installed from here If Saxon is not already installed, Saxon-HE can be downloaded and installed from here Saxon (Java) tasks need the path to the installed Saxon jar file to be included in a property in your Visual Studio Code settings. Invoke Preferences: Open User Settings from the Command Palette (⇧⌘P) and type xslt to show a short-list of settings: Enter the Jar file path in the Tasks: Saxon Jar property (see above)"},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Saxon-JS Tasks","url":"run-xslt.html#saxonjs-tasks","text":"Ensure that you have a workspace folder open in Visual Studio Code (use File > open... from the menu). The configuration will be saved in a tasks.json file in this workspace folder. If NodeJS is not already installed, it can be downloaded and installed from here Initialize NPM in the current folder and then install the Saxon-JS package (xslt3) by running the following commands in the Visual Studio Code terminal (⌘J): npm init -y npm install --save-dev xslt3 The Saxon-JS task uses NPX to invoke Saxon-JS task. NPX will download the Saxon-JS package if you choose not to follow the previous step and it can't find the Saxon-JS package in your global space."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"SaxonC Tasks","url":"run-xslt.html#saxonc-tasks","text":"A new xslt-c task type runs XSLT transforms using SaxonC, Saxonica's native C\/C++ port of Saxon, alongside the existing xslt (Saxon Java) and xslt-js (Saxon-JS) task types. Its command-line arguments mirror the Saxon (Java) task closely, since SaxonC's Transform command-line tool follows the same conventions."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Setup","url":"run-xslt.html#setup","text":"Install SaxonC and note the folder containing its Transform (or Transform.exe on Windows) command-line executable. Set the XSLT.tasks.saxonCPath user setting to that folder. Invoke Tasks: Run Build Task from the Command Palette (⇧⌘B) and configure a new xslt-c task, following the same steps as for a Saxon (Java) task described above. SaxonC tasks can be disabled with the XSLT.tasks.saxonc.enabled setting, matching the equivalent settings for Saxon (Java) and Saxon-JS tasks."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Native library path (macOS\/Linux)","url":"run-xslt.html#native-library-path-macos-linux","text":"Some SaxonC distributions are built without an embedded library search path, so the native shared libraries alongside Transform - and any others it depends on - may not be found automatically. The xslt-c task always searches the lib folder next to the configured saxonCPath, and you can list additional folders (for example, a separately-packaged core library) in the saxonCLibraryPaths task property, or the equivalent XSLT.tasks.saxonCLibraryPaths user setting. If a transform fails with an error like Library not loaded: ...dylib ... no LC_RPATH's found, add the folder containing that library to saxonCLibraryPaths."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Licensing","url":"run-xslt.html#licensing","text":"If SaxonC reports No license file found - running with licensable features disabled, set the licenseFileLocation task property (or the equivalent XSLT.tasks.saxonCLicenseFileLocation user setting) to the path of your saxon-license.lic file. This is passed to Transform as --licenseFileLocation:, the same option Saxon (Java) tasks use."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"To configure the initial task:","url":"run-xslt.html#to-configure-the-initial-task","text":"Invoke Tasks: Run Build Task from the Command Palette (⇧⌘B): Press Enter to Configure Build Task: Select from the list (shown above) the task type you require (you may see more task types in the list), for either Saxon or Saxon-JS. The tasks.json file will then be opened with a template task added for the task type specified. The task shown above is for the Saxon task. Edit this task to fit your XSLT transform requirement. It's a good idea to change the label property (the value shown in the tasks list) to describe your XSLT transform. Auto-completion with hints is available as you type. The actual file type is 'JSON with Comments' so you can add comments and leave trailing commas etc. You can create any number of tasks for different XSLT transform scenarios. Note that the group.isDefault property controls that task that is automatically launched when you next invoke Run Build Task from the Command Palette or when using ⇧⌘B. Save any changes to the tasks.json file ⌘S Run the newly configured XSLT transform by invoking Run Build Task from the Command Palette or when using ⇧⌘B."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Variable References in Tasks","url":"run-xslt.html#references","text":"Visual Studio Code supports variable substitutions for Task configuration files. Variable substitution uses the ${variableName} syntax. Commands that return a string can be invoked via variable substitution, the boiler plate XSLT Task uses ${command:xslt-xpath.pickXsltFile}' and ${command:xslt-xpath.pickResultFile}' variable references."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"User-input for file-selection within a Task","url":"run-xslt.html#user-input-for-file-selection-within-a-task","text":"A set of 'Pick File' commands suitable for XSLT tasks is available to allow user-selected file paths to be used within an XSLT task. An example is shown below: Each command prompts the user to select a file. This is from: the current file, a 'recent files' list for that command (optionally from a specified 'previous stage' command), or from a 'Open File' or 'Save File' VS Code dialog as appropriate. The available commands are listed below: Command Description xslt-xpath.pickFile General file selection with arguments to provide custom behaviour. This must be invoked with arguments that are supplied in the 'inputs' property of the tasks.json file. Invoked by ${input:<input-id>} xslt-xpath.pickXsltFile Returns a user-selected XSLT file xslt-xpath.pickXmlSourceFile Returns a user-selected XML Source file xslt-xpath.pickStage2XmlSourceFile Returns a user-selected Stage2 XML source file xslt-xpath.pickResultFile Returns a user-selected result file xslt-xpath.pickStage2ResultFile Returns a user-selected result file for Stage2 A full list of variable references is given in Visual Studio Code's Variables Reference"},{"page":"Tasks","section":"The 'pickFile' command","url":"run-xslt.html#the-pickfile-command","text":"The general purpose command 'xslt-xpath.pickFile' is the base commands for specific 'pick file' commands like 'pickXsltFile'. After a task is invoked, the user is prompted to select a file for opening or saving. A Pick List is shown with options to select files in up to 5 ways: the current file (if it's extension is compatible with the extensions list) from recently used (current stage) files - from list for the current label e.g. 'Select XSLT File' from recently used (previous stage result) files - shows result files from a previous task from the <?xml-stylesheet href=\"...?> href in the current file from an 'Open File' or 'Save File' dialog The 'recently used' file data is maintained per workspace and is restored when VS Code is closed and reopened. A 'clear recently used' option is also listed when there are files already in the list. The 'pickList' command must be invoked as an 'input' property of a task as it requires an 'options' argument. A sample 'inputs' property can be added to the 'tasks.json' file with the XSLT: add Inputs to Task file command."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Command Options for 'pickFile'","url":"run-xslt.html#command-options-for-pickfile","text":"The 'pickFile' commands 'options' object have the following properties: label - the prompt message for the user, also the identifier for the 'recently' used list extensions? - an array of filter file-extensions (when set, a '*' option can also be selected from the dialog) isResult? - causes the 'Save File' dialog to be shown when set true prevStageLabel? - shows the recently used files for the specified label previousStageGroup? - the group-name for the 'previous stage' files - default: 'recent files from previous stage' xmlStylesheetPI? - set 'true' for pick-list to include a <?xml-stylesheet?> href from current editor Sample input property definitions for the 'pickFile' command are listed below: \"inputs\": [ \/* 1. the specialist 'pickFile' command equivalents for the inputs defined here are: \"xsltFile\": \"${command:xslt-xpath.pickXsltFile}\", \/\/ can select from current file - if xslt - or recently used xslt files \"xmlSource\": \"${command:xslt-xpath.pickXmlSourceFile}\", \/\/ can select from current file or recently used stage1 source files \"xmlSource\": \"${command:xslt-xpath.pickStage2XmlSourceFile}\", \/\/ can select from recently used stage2 source files or stage1 result files \"resultPath\": \"${command:xslt-xpath.pickResultFile}\", \/\/ can save to recently used stage1 result files \"resultPath\": \"${command:xslt-xpath.pickStage2ResultFile}\", \/\/ can save to recently used stage2 result files 2. these inputs invoke the 'pickFile' command with args for custom behaviour: *\/ { \/* --- Usage: --- \"xmlSource\": \"${input:xmlFile}\", *\/ \"id\": \"xmlFile\", \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"xslt-xpath.pickFile\", \"args\": {\"label\": \"Select XML File\", \"extensions\": [\"xml\", \"xhtml\", \"svg\"] } }, { \/* --- Usage: --- \"xmlSource\": \"${input:xmlFile2}\", *\/ \"id\": \"xmlFile2\", \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"xslt-xpath.pickFile\", \"args\": {\"label\": \"Select Stage1 XML File\", \"extensions\": [\"xml\", \"xhtml\", \"svg\"], \"prevStageLabel\": \"Select Result File\", \"prevStageGroup\": \"recent files from previous stage\" } }, { \/* --- Usage: --- \"xsltFile\": \"${input:xsltFile}\", *\/ \"id\": \"xsltFile\", \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"xslt-xpath.pickFile\", \"args\": {\"label\": \"Select XSLT Stylesheet\", \"extensions\": [\"xsl\", \"xslt\"], \"xmlStylesheetPI\": true } }, { \/* --- Usage: --- \"resultPath\": \"${input:resultFile}\", *\/ \"id\": \"resultFile\", \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"xslt-xpath.pickFile\", \"args\": {\"label\": \"Select Result File\", \"isResult\": true } } ]"},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Configuring more tasks","url":"run-xslt.html#configuring-more-tasks","text":"To create more tasks, just invoke Tasks: Run Build Task from the Command Palette and choose the task type you need. Provided you've changed the label property from the template task for that type, a new task will created from the template task. Visual Studio Tasks are very flexible. You can combine XSLT tasks with other task types, for example, to run a shell command. The dependsOn task property is used to chain tasks together. The Compound Tasks section of the Visual Studio Code Tasks documentation covers this concept in more detail."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"XML catalogs","url":"run-xslt.html#catalogs","text":"The xslt and xslt-c tasks pass the catalog of the XSLT.resources.catalog setting to Saxon, with the -catalog option - unless the task has its own catalogFilenames property. See XML Catalogs."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"Java extension functions","url":"run-xslt.html#java-extension-functions","text":"An xslt task can run a stylesheet that calls Java code. Add the folder or jar with your classes to the task's classPathEntries property. Saxon supports two kinds of Java extension function: Reflexive - a namespace with a java: URI names a Java class, and the functions in it call the class's methods, e.g. math:sqrt(2) with xmlns:math=\"java:java.lang.Math\". These need Saxon-PE or Saxon-EE. Integrated - a class that extends Saxon's ExtensionFunctionDefinition, registered by a Saxon configuration file. Set the task's configFilename property to the file. These work with any Saxon edition, including Saxon-HE. \"classPathEntries\": [\"${workspaceFolder}\/lib\/my-functions.jar\"], \"configFilename\": \"${workspaceFolder}\/saxon-config.xml\" The configuration file names each integrated function's class: <configuration xmlns=\"http:\/\/saxon.sf.net\/ns\/configuration\" edition=\"HE\"> <resources> <extensionFunction>com.example.Shout<\/extensionFunction> <\/resources> <\/configuration> The debugger runs the stylesheet with the task's properties, so Java extension functions work when debugging too - including in Watch expressions. The linter doesn't check calls of a java: namespace's functions. For an integrated function, add its prefix to the XSLT.validation.externalFunctionPrefixes setting - the quick fix for a function that isn't found does this - and calls of functions with that prefix aren't checked."},{"page":"Tasks","section":"The Visual Studio Code terminal","url":"run-xslt.html#the-visual-studio-code-terminal","text":"When you start a Task, it is run in a Task tab in Visual Studio Code's Integrated Terminal, the Pane hosting the Terminal view is toggle with ⌘J. The tab label reflects the Task configuration label property. When a Saxon XSLT task is run, xsl:message output and any Saxon errors will be shown in the terminal window. The above screenshot shows formatted and colorised xsl:message output available through special auto-completion options. See the Debugging section for more detail."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"","url":"debugger.html","text":"XSLT stylesheets can be debugged with SaxonJ in VS Code's debugger: set breakpoints, step through templates, functions and multi-line XPath expressions, and inspect variables, the context item and the output written so far. If you're new to debugging in VS Code, see Debugging in the VS Code documentation for the debug toolbar, views and keyboard shortcuts. For debugging by adding xsl:message instructions to the stylesheet, see Debugging with xsl:message. Requirements: SaxonJ 12 or later - any edition, including Saxon-HE - set with the XSLT.tasks.saxonJar setting, and a Java runtime on the PATH. Debugging with SaxonJS or SaxonC isn't supported. For XPath 4.0 syntax in an XSLT 4.0 stylesheet, Saxon-PE or Saxon-EE is needed."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Starting a debug session","url":"debugger.html#starting","text":"Open the stylesheet and set a breakpoint - click in the margin to the left of a line number, or press F9. Then start debugging in any of these ways: the Debug Stylesheet with SaxonJ button in the editor title bar XSLT: Debug Stylesheet with SaxonJ from the Command Palette F5, when there's no launch.json - or the Debug Stylesheet link in the Run and Debug view The stylesheet runs as Quick Run runs it: with the XML context file, or from xsl:initial-template when there's no context file. It uses the same Quick Run task, so parameters added to that task also apply when debugging - if there's more than one task for the stylesheet and context file, you pick which. When the stylesheet is imported or included by another, you're offered the choice of running the parent stylesheet instead. A Quick Run task includes the stylesheet's required parameters, with values from tasks.json inputs. You're asked for these values when the debug session starts, as when the task runs. If a task refers to an input that tasks.json doesn't define, the session doesn't start, and you're offered Open tasks.json. The Run and Debug View provides options for how to launch the debugger."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Launch configurations","url":"debugger.html#launch-json","text":"With no launch.json, the Run and Debug view offers Debug Active Stylesheet, and a Debug task label configuration for each xslt task in tasks.json (see XSLT: Tasks). create a launch.json file adds these to a new launch.json. To debug a task without a launch.json, choose XSLT (Saxon)... in the Run and Debug view's dropdown, or use Debug: Select and Start Debugging from the Command Palette, then the task's Debug task label. The configuration stays selected in the dropdown, so F5 debugs that task again - whichever file is open. A launch configuration uses the xslt-saxon debugger type, with one of: \"quickRun\": true - the stylesheet in the active editor, as Quick Run runs it \"task\" - the label of an xslt task in tasks.json, whose properties are used; properties set in the configuration override them the same properties as an xslt task, such as xsltFile, xmlSource, initialTemplate, resultPath and parameters { \"type\": \"xslt-saxon\", \"request\": \"launch\", \"name\": \"Debug report\", \"task\": \"report with books.xml\", \"stopOnEntry\": false } \"stopOnEntry\": true pauses at the first instruction executed. In launch.json, Add Configuration... offers four snippets: XSLT (Saxon): Debug Active Stylesheet, Debug a Task, Debug Stylesheet (with a source file) and Debug from Initial Template. See Debug configuration in the VS Code documentation for more on launch.json."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Breakpoints","url":"debugger.html#breakpoints","text":"A breakpoint can be set on a line where an XSLT instruction or a literal result element starts. A breakpoint anywhere in a multi-line start tag is moved to the tag's first line. Breakpoints can also be set inside a multi-line XPath expression - see Stepping through XPath."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Conditional breakpoints, hit counts and logpoints","url":"debugger.html#conditional","text":"Right-click in the margin and choose Add Conditional Breakpoint... or Add Logpoint... - or right-click an existing breakpoint and choose Edit Breakpoint: Expression - an XPath expression, evaluated where the breakpoint is, with its variables and context item, e.g. @customer = 'Grace' or $total gt 30. If the expression raises an error, execution pauses and the error is shown. Hit Count - 5 (the 5th hit), >5, >=5, <5, <=5 or %5 (every 5th hit). With an expression too, only the hits where the expression is true are counted. Log Message - a logpoint writes the message to the Debug Console without pausing. Each {xpath} in the message is replaced by its value, e.g. {$line\/@product} costs {f:line-total($line)}. Logging all variables: {$*} is a special case - it's replaced by the name and value of each local variable in scope at the logpoint, one per line, e.g. order {@id}: {$*}. It's like an xsl:message with xdm:debug(), but without changing the stylesheet. Setting a conditional breakpoint with an XPath expression."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Function and named-template breakpoints","url":"debugger.html#function-breakpoints","text":"To stop whenever a stylesheet function or named template is called, without finding where it's declared, click + (Add Function Breakpoint) in the Breakpoints view and enter its name. The function or template can be in any module of the stylesheet, including imported and included modules. Execution stops at the start of the function or template, where the Variables view shows its parameters - for a named template, these are the values passed to it. The name is written as it is where the function or template is declared, with the same prefix, e.g. f:total or cell - or as an EQName, e.g. Q{urn:f}total. A () after the name is allowed. An arity selects one version of a function, e.g. f:total#2. With no arity, every version of the function stops. Conditions and hit counts work as for line breakpoints - right-click the breakpoint and choose Edit Condition or Edit Hit Count. The condition can use the parameters, e.g. $n = 2. A name that isn't valid is shown as unverified straight away. A function or template that isn't in the stylesheet - or a function with no version of the arity given - is shown as unverified once the stylesheet has compiled; hover over the breakpoint for the reason. Template rules - templates with a match attribute - aren't stopped at this way, even when they also have a name: use a line breakpoint instead."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Pausing on errors","url":"debugger.html#errors","text":"The Breakpoints view has two options for errors: Dynamic Errors (on by default) - pauses where a dynamic error that ends the transform is raised, including xsl:message with terminate=\"yes\". The error's code and message are shown, and the variables at that point can be inspected. Errors Caught by xsl:try (off by default) - also pauses at errors that an xsl:try catches. This needs Saxon 12.5 or later. The debugger paused on a dynamic error. A static error - one Saxon finds when it compiles the stylesheet - can't be paused at, as the stylesheet never runs. This includes a type error that Saxon can tell will always happen: for example, an xsl:variable with as=\"xs:string\" whose select is count(...), which is always an xs:integer. The session then ends with a notification that gives the first error and its line, and the errors are listed in the Problems view."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Stepping","url":"debugger.html#stepping","text":"The debug toolbar has the usual Continue (F5), Step Over (F10), Step Into (F11), Step Out (⇧F11), Pause and Stop commands. In XSLT terms: Step Over goes to the next line in the current template or function, without stopping in the templates and functions it calls - for example, with xsl:apply-templates or xsl:call-template. Step Into stops in each template rule, named template or function called, and in a global variable when it's evaluated. Step Out continues until the current template or function returns. The bodies of xsl:for-each, xsl:for-each-group and xsl:iterate are stepped through, not over, for each item. Stepping goes line by line: execution stops once each time it reaches a line, not once for each instruction on it. A breakpoint in a loop stops once for each iteration."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Stepping through XPath","url":"debugger.html#xpath","text":"A multi-line XPath expression in a select, test or value attribute - or in xsl:map-entry's key, xsl:evaluate's xpath, context-item or with-params, or the content of an XSLT 4.0 xsl:select - can be stepped through. An xsl:when's test is stopped at where it's evaluated: only the tests reached, in order, until one is true. In XSLT 4.0, the select of an xsl:when or xsl:otherwise, and an xsl:if's then and else, are stepped through only when that branch is taken; an xsl:record's attributes - its fields' expressions - are stepped through as the record is built. Within xsl:catch, the $err: variables can be used, as in Watch. An xsl:with-param's select is stepped through as part of the instruction it's in, e.g. xsl:call-template. An xsl:sort's select is stopped at for each item, as its sort key is computed - before the first item is processed - with that item as the context item. Use Step Into at the instruction, or set a breakpoint on a line of the expression. Execution stops on each line where a part of the expression starts. Step Into goes through an expression when that shows more than its result: when it binds variables (for, let, some or every), calls a stylesheet function or an inline function, or has an if whose branches are on lines of their own - execution then stops at the branch taken. Otherwise - for example, for comparisons joined by or - Step Into steps over it, and the variable's value is shown when it's in scope. A breakpoint on a line of the expression still stops there. A simple map (!), a path step (\/) or a predicate ([...]) is evaluated for each item of the expression before it. A breakpoint on a line of its right-hand side, or predicate, stops for each item, with that item as the context item and its position() and last() in the Context section. Step Into goes through such a loop only when what's evaluated for each item has something to show, as above - e.g. a function call - so that it doesn't stop for each item of a long path. A function argument that Saxon evaluates lazily - within the function, when it's first used - shows its context item, but position() and last() as (not known). The expression is evaluated separately, in the instruction's context, so that each part reports its own line. It appears in the call stack as, for example, xsl:variable\/@select (XPath), and its result is shown in the Debug Console, e.g. ▸ $line-totals (xsl:variable\/@select, lines 25-28) returned (6.75, 10.8, 15.75) - or the dynamic error it raised, e.g. one an xsl:try catches. The instruction itself then runs as usual, but without stopping again in the functions the expression calls, or writing their xsl:message output again - so each is stopped in, and its messages written, once. The body of an inline function is stepped through each time it's called - for example, by fold-left(). Evaluation context and call stack in the Run and Debug view when execution is paused."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Call stack","url":"debugger.html#call-stack","text":"The Call Stack view has a frame for each: template rule, with its match pattern and mode, e.g. xsl:template match=\"order\" mode=\"summary\" named template, e.g. xsl:template name=\"header\" function, by name and arity, e.g. f:line-total#1 global variable or parameter being evaluated, e.g. xsl:variable $orders xsl:for-each, xsl:for-each-group, xsl:iterate or xsl:analyze-string instruction, with its select expression XPath expression being stepped through, e.g. xsl:variable\/@select (XPath) Selecting a frame shows its location in the editor, and its variables in the Variables view."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Variables","url":"debugger.html#variables","text":"The Variables view has four sections: Locals - the parameters of the template, function or xsl:iterate, then the variables in scope - from xsl:variable, and from XPath for, let, some and every expressions, including a positional at $pos variable. Tunnel parameters are shown as $name (tunnel). A value that Saxon hasn't evaluated yet, and whose evaluation would run stylesheet code - for example, with Saxon 12, the accumulator passed to a fold-left function - is shown as (not evaluated yet), with a button to evaluate it. Evaluating it runs that code straight away, without stopping at its breakpoints. Context - the context item (.), position() and last(), and where they apply, current-group(), current-grouping-key() and each regex-group(). Globals - the global variables and parameters, sorted by name. A global variable that hasn't been used yet is evaluated when it's shown. While a global variable is being evaluated, one that might use it - directly, or through a function - isn't evaluated, and is shown as (not evaluated yet): evaluating it would be a circular reference. Output - which result is being written to, or that a temporary tree is being built (for example, an xsl:variable's content), and the end of each output written so far. Nodes, maps, arrays and sequences can be expanded. An element shows its attributes, then its child nodes, with the line number of the element in its source document. Long values are truncated, and an expanded value shows at most 1000 items."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Inline values","url":"debugger.html#inline-values","text":"While paused, the value of each variable declared earlier in the current template or function is shown at the end of the line where it's declared. This applies to xsl:variable and xsl:param, and in XPath, to let, for, some and every variables and inline function parameters. The VS Code setting debug.inlineValues turns this off."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Watch, hover and the Debug Console","url":"debugger.html#evaluating","text":"While paused, type an XPath expression at the prompt at the bottom of the Debug Console and press Enter to evaluate it - for example, line[@qty gt 1]\/@product on an order element, or count(current-group()) in xsl:for-each-group. Expressions added to the Watch view are evaluated in the same way, and again each time execution pauses. Expressions are evaluated in the context of the call stack frame selected in the Call Stack view, with: the variables in scope, and the context item the stylesheet's namespace declarations and functions, e.g. f:line-total($line) current(), current-group(), current-grouping-key() and regex-group(), which give their values where execution is paused Results are shown, and can be expanded, as in the Variables view. Hovering over a variable reference such as $total, or the name in a variable declaration, shows its value."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Output while debugging","url":"debugger.html#output","text":"The output written so far - the principal result, and each xsl:result-document - opens beside the stylesheet at the first pause where there is some, and is updated at each pause. It's serialized by Saxon as the result is, but indented for readability, and shown in the language of its output method: XML, HTML, JSON or plain text. The result itself is written as the stylesheet specifies. XSLT: Show Debug Output, or its button on the debug toolbar, opens the output at any time. When there's more than one output, you choose which to show. The XSLT.debug.openOutput setting turns off opening the output automatically. xsl:message output, and the result when there's no resultPath, are written to the Debug Console. When the session ends, a notification offers to Open the result file - or, for HTML, to Open in Browser."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Saxon errors","url":"debugger.html#saxon-errors","text":"Saxon's error messages in the Debug Console are linked to the location of the error. The errors are also listed in the Problems view, as for XSLT tasks. A file's errors are removed from the Problems view when the file is edited, as they may no longer apply. When the transform fails, the notification at the end of the session gives Saxon's reason - for example, that the XML source file doesn't exist - or, for a dynamic error, its message, code and line. For a stylesheet with static errors, which can't be debugged, it gives the first of them."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Sample: stepping through XPath","url":"debugger.html#demo","text":"The debugger-demo sample has a stylesheet, debug-xpath.xsl, and an XML file, orders.xml. Open orders.xml, so it becomes the XML context file, then debug-xpath.xsl, and start debugging. Things to try are listed in a comment at the top of the stylesheet, including: stepping through a multi-line for ... return expression, and into a function it calls a breakpoint inside an expression in xsl:for-each-group, with count(current-group()) in the Watch view conditional breakpoints, hit counts and a logpoint pausing at a dynamic error, after changing a price in orders.xml to n\/a"},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Limitations","url":"debugger.html#limitations","text":"For debugging, the stylesheet is compiled without optimization, so all variables can be inspected. Saxon still evaluates constant expressions when it compiles - e.g. an xsl:if whose test uses only constant values - and an instruction removed that way can't be stopped at. Values can't be changed in the Variables view. An expression that's stepped through is evaluated in full - except a test, e.g. an xsl:if's, which is evaluated only as far as needed for true or false, as Saxon does. Where Saxon itself would evaluate only part of it - e.g. an xsl:iterate's select, when xsl:break ends the loop, or a variable that's only partly used - stepping through it can call more functions, and so write more xsl:message output, than a normal run. With \"messageEscaping\": \"on\" in the xslt task, Saxon writes xsl:message output itself, so a message from a function called by an expression that's stepped through is written twice: once as the expression is stepped through, and again as the instruction runs. Only modules loaded from files can be stopped in. Saxon only evaluates xsl:assert with assertions enabled - \"enableAssertions\": \"on\" in the xslt task - so without that, its breakpoints are unverified. Saxon evaluates some instructions lazily - e.g. an xsl:for-each in an xsl:map runs when the map is built - so the call stack may not show them while their content is stopped in. The context item is still the right one. Accumulators: Saxon computes an accumulator's values for the whole document the first time one of them is needed - e.g. by accumulator-before() - so a breakpoint in an xsl:accumulator-rule stops for each node in turn at that point, not as each node is processed. If a breakpoint condition or a Watch expression is the first to need the values, they're computed while it's evaluated, and the rule's breakpoints aren't stopped at. Accumulator rules are reported to the debugger by Saxon 12.8 or later. Keys: Saxon builds a key's index for the whole document the first time key() is called for it - so a breakpoint on an xsl:key stops for each node indexed at that point. As for accumulators, if a breakpoint condition or Watch expression is the first to need the index, it's built while that's evaluated, and the key's breakpoints aren't stopped at."},{"page":"Debugger","section":"Troubleshooting","url":"debugger.html#troubleshooting","text":"When a debug session doesn't start, a notification gives the reason, with a Show Output button - for example, when Java isn't on the PATH, the Saxon jar doesn't exist, or the Java runtime is too old for the Saxon jar. The XSLT Debugger log, in the Output panel, shows how each session was launched: the Java command line, the Saxon product, the Java runtime and the Saxon options used."},{"page":"Print Debugging","section":"","url":"code-diagnostics.html","text":"To set breakpoints and step through a stylesheet, see the XSLT Debugger. The debugger also supports logpoints for printing debug values and text to the debug console - this avoids the need to modify your XSLT but provides less flexibility than xsl:message Adding xsl:message instructions is another way to debug XSLT - one that works with any Saxon processor, including SaxonJS and SaxonC, and shows how values change over a whole run. The output is sent to the terminal window. Diagnostics are added using the xdm:debug() function, from the xdm-viewer XSLT library, which renders one or more named in-scope variables to a formatted string for use in an xsl:message."},{"page":"Print Debugging","section":"Diagnosing state of in-scope XSLT variables","url":"code-diagnostics.html#diagnosing-state-of-in-scope-xslt-variables","text":"There is special provision for conveniently reviewing the state of in-scope XSLT variables. This approach is outlined here."},{"page":"Print Debugging","section":"1. Use Auto-complete for xsl:message","url":"code-diagnostics.html#1-use-auto-complete-for-xsl-message","text":"To assist with debugging, the XSLT editor has special auto-completion for the xsl:message instruction. Four completion items are offered when in-scope variables are found: (blank) - inserts a simple placeholder, for entering your own xsl:message content adaptive serialization - serializes last declared variable using serialize() with the adaptive method xdm:debug variables - lists all in-scope variables in a single xdm:debug() call xdm:debug-color variables - as above, with ANSI-colored terminal output The auto-complete list for xsl:message when variables or parameters are in context."},{"page":"Print Debugging","section":"2. Review Inserted xsl:message","url":"code-diagnostics.html#2-review-inserted-xsl-message","text":"When xdm:debug variables (or xdm:debug-color variables) is selected from the auto-complete list, an xsl:message snippet is inserted at the cursor with all in-scope variables collected into a single XPath map, passed as the second argument to xdm:debug(). A title, defaulting to the name of the enclosing xsl:function or the mode of an enclosing xsl:template, is inserted as the first argument. <xsl:message select=\"xdm:debug('Watch: my-function', map { 'items': $items, 'total': $total })\"\/> The generated xsl:message inserted with unresolved call to xdm:debug-color()"},{"page":"Print Debugging","section":"3. Quick Fix: Include the xdm-view Library","url":"code-diagnostics.html#3-quick-fix-include-the-xdm-view-library","text":"As can be seen above, the xdm:debug() function is not initially resolvable - it will be marked as a 'problem' until its defining library is included. Hover over the problem function and accept the 'QuickFix' by pressing Enter. Two Quick Fix options are offered: Include XSLT module for xdm:debug (the default, preferred option) - inserts the xdm namespace declaration (http:\/\/deltaxignia.com\/ns\/xdm-persistence) and an xsl:include instruction referencing a workspace-local copy of xdm-view.xsl if one is found, otherwise the copy of the xdm-viewer library bundled with this extension. Copy xdm-view library into workspace - copies the bundled xdm-viewer library (and its xdm-persistence dependency) into an xslt-resources\/xdm-view folder in your workspace, then applies the same include as above. Use this if you want the library files available locally, for example to inspect or version them alongside your own stylesheets. Quick Fix menu to resolve the call to xdm:debug-color()"},{"page":"Print Debugging","section":"4. Run XSLT and Review State in Terminal","url":"code-diagnostics.html#4-run-xslt-and-review-state-in-terminal","text":"You can now run the XSLT as normal, but you will now see the xsl:message output in the terminal window, with each in-scope variable shown under a title banner. Node values are shown with their location path and a pruned, truncated rendering, keeping the output readable for large trees. xsl:message output xdm:debug-color(). Colorising for xdm:debug-color() should work from VSCode with all SaxonJ, SaxonC and SaxonJS versions. Because later SaxonJ versions don't support use of a built-in TextEmitter command-line option, an extension class is used instead of `net.sf.saxon.Transform` when invoking a transform via an XSLT task. This prevents escaping of ANSI codes used for coloring the output. For this same reason, in SaxonC, the XML output from SaxonC is decoded prior to output to the terminal."},{"page":"Print Debugging","section":"5. Benefits of xdm:debug() over fn:serialize()","url":"code-diagnostics.html#5-benefits-of-xdm-debug-over-fn-serialize","text":"We recommend the xdm:debug-color() function as it supplements the built-in serialize function with features that are especially important when you're interested in several variable values or the values have complex types. The auto-complete fills in the arguments, so you rarely need to change them. The function signatures are: xdm:debug($title as xs:string, $labels as map(xs:string, item()*)) as xs:string xdm:debug($title as xs:string, $labels as map(xs:string, item()*), $level as xs:integer) as xs:string The output begins with a title to help locate the xsl:message in the code The remaining content is structured in two columns: variable names in the first, values in the second Nested maps, arrays and sequences are formatted for readability Bracket-pair coloring helps identify the start\/end of maps, sequences or arrays A set of named variables or parameters can be serialized with a single function Lexical XPath locations are shown above each node, and above each XPath 4.0 JNode, e.g. \/lines\/*[2]\/sku - see JSON and JNodes Node content is truncated when required to keep message output concise Syntax highlighting helps identify types and map keys etc. Values are annotated with their type where this is non-obvious The optional $level argument adds indentation proportional to this value - helpful for recursive calls"},{"page":"XPath Notebooks","section":"","url":"xpath-notebook.html","text":"This section describes DeltaXml's XPath Notebook extension for Visual Studio Code. Here we cover the fundamentals of using XPath 3.1 in a Visual Studio Code Notebook."},{"page":"XPath Notebooks","section":"Why use an XPath Notebook?","url":"xpath-notebook.html#why-use-an-xpath-notebook","text":"XPath Notebooks are used for a wide range of purposes, including: Quick analysis of XML or JSON Learning and experimenting with XPath Tutorials on XPath Tutorials on a specific XML or JSON format"},{"page":"XPath Notebooks","section":"Quick Start","url":"xpath-notebook.html#quick-start","text":"For this guide, we're assuming you've already installed the XPath Notebook extension for VS Code."},{"page":"XPath Notebooks","section":"1. Install Node.js","url":"xpath-notebook.html#1-install-node-js","text":"This tool uses a builtin SaxonJS XSLT processor from Saxonica. This processor requires a recent installation of Node.js. Available for download from https:\/\/nodejs.org\/en\/."},{"page":"XPath Notebooks","section":"2. Select an XML 'source' file","url":"xpath-notebook.html#2-select-an-xml-source-file","text":"Open any XML (or JSON) source file in VS Code to set this as the context item for XPath expressions in an XPath Notebook. The most recent XML\/JSON file opened is used."},{"page":"XPath Notebooks","section":"3. Create and save a new XPath Notebook","url":"xpath-notebook.html#3-create-and-save-a-new-xpath-notebook","text":"From Visual Studio Code, open the Command Palette (⇧⌘P) and execute the New XPath Notebook command. An untitled notebook with a Markdown cell and a Code cell will be shown, initially the file is untitled with a .xbook file extension. Save this notebook in the usual way (⌘S), keeping the .xbook extension. Note: In the 1.65 VS Code release, an empty XPath notebook is created in addition to a XPath notebook with a pre-populated Markdown cell (the API for creating new notebooks is not fully resolved yet)."},{"page":"XPath Notebooks","section":"4. Edit and execute a new code cell","url":"xpath-notebook.html#4-edit-and-execute-a-new-code-cell","text":"To add a new XPath code cell, press the + Code button at the top of the notebook. In the new code cell, enter the \/* XPath expression, then press ^Enter to execute the cell. The result cell is shown immediately below the Code cell."},{"page":"AI Assistants","section":"","url":"ai-assistants.html","text":"AI coding assistants in Visual Studio Code - such as GitHub Copilot or Claude Code - can make good use of the XSLT\/XPath extension. They don't need anything special from it: the extension's problems, documentation notes and debugging output are all in forms that assistants read and write well."},{"page":"AI Assistants","section":"Fixing problems","url":"ai-assistants.html#problems","text":"The problems reported by the extension's linter are standard Visual Studio Code diagnostics, shown in the Problems panel. They identify the exact symbol at issue - an unresolved variable, a function called with the wrong number of arguments, a record field that doesn't exist - so an assistant that reads the diagnostics can explain and fix them precisely, and check its own changes the same way. For example, GitHub Copilot can include the problems with #problems in a chat prompt, or fix a problem from its Quick Fix menu, and Claude Code reads them through its Visual Studio Code integration."},{"page":"AI Assistants","section":"Documentation notes","url":"ai-assistants.html#notes","text":"XSLT 4.0 documentation notes - xsl:note format=\"xdoc-md\" - use Markdown with @param and @return tags, in the style of Javadoc: a format that assistants already know. An assistant can read a function's note to understand it, and can write notes too - for example, when asked to document the functions in a stylesheet. As the extension checks the @param tags against the parameters, a missing or misnamed parameter in a note is reported straight away."},{"page":"AI Assistants","section":"Debugging output","url":"ai-assistants.html#debugging","text":"The output of xdm:debug() in xsl:message is plain, labelled text: each variable's value in XPath syntax, with its type where it isn't obvious, and each node with its path. Copied from the terminal into a chat, it gives an assistant the actual values to reason about, rather than a description of the problem."},{"page":"AI Assistants","section":"Running XSLT","url":"ai-assistants.html#running","text":"Quick Run creates ordinary tasks in the workspace's tasks.json file, and each task shows the Saxon command line it runs in the terminal. An assistant that can run commands can run a transform in the same way."},{"page":"AI Assistants","section":"Current limitations","url":"ai-assistants.html#limitations","text":"The extension doesn't yet provide an MCP server or language model tools, so an assistant can't ask it directly for information - for example, to check a file that isn't open, to find where a symbol is declared, to list the fields of a record type, or to show the imports of a module. Assistants that run outside Visual Studio Code, such as command-line agents, only see the extension's problems if they can read Visual Studio Code's diagnostics. Tools for assistants may be added in a future release."},{"page":"XSLT 3.0","section":"","url":"xslt30.html","text":"XSLT 3.0, published by W3c on 8 June 2017, is the latest stable version of XSLT. As such, it is the target version for XSLT in this extension, alongside XPath 3.1."},{"page":"XSLT 3.0","section":"Learning XSLT 3.0","url":"xslt30.html#learning-xslt-3-0","text":"The online W3C documentation for XSLT 3.0, along with its XPath 3.1 sub-language, comprises 3 main parts: XSL Transformations (XSLT) Version 3.0 XML Path Language (XPath) 3.1 XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1 Note: The XSLT 3.1 Serialization specification is published separately as this is shared with XQuery. While the size of each part of the XSLT specifications may appear daunting at first, each part is well-structured, with a Table of Contents panel that makes it easy to navigate. Many code samples are included with the specification."},{"page":"XSLT 3.0","section":"Offline Reading Material","url":"xslt30.html#offline-reading-material","text":"There are no published books for XSLT 3.0, but the XSLT 2.0 and XPath 2.0 Programmer's Reference by Michael Kay is frequently recommended. XSLT 3.0 is a significant enhancement to XSLT 2.0 but it builds on the fundamental principals and language in XSLT 2.0."},{"page":"XSLT 3.0","section":"Video Tutorials","url":"xslt30.html#video-tutorials","text":"A set of video tutorials by Dimitre Novatchev is available: XSLT 2.0 and 1.0 Foundations. The Evolution of XPath: What’s New in XPath 3.0."},{"page":"XSLT 3.0","section":"Supporting XSLT Processors","url":"xslt30.html#supporting-xslt-processors","text":"This extension integrates with the Saxon XSLT processor by Saxonica via XSLT tasks through the Saxon command-line interface. Saxon is probably the most popular\/ubiquitous XSLT 3.0 processor. It publishes specification conformance data and supports many platforms. A free open source version Saxon-HE is available along with paid-for 'Professional' and 'Enterprise' variants are also available. These are summarised in a feature matrix. Saxon PE and EE 12.8 or later, run with syntax extensions, also accept some XSLT 4.0 features in an XSLT 3.0 stylesheet: named item types - with record and enumeration types - and xsl:note. To edit such stylesheets with the same support as for XSLT 4.0, enable the XSLT.validation.xslt30ItemTypesAndNotes setting - see XSLT 4.0 Item Types and Notes in XSLT 3.0. For the full set of XSLT 4.0 features implemented in Saxon 13, see XSLT 4.0. Altova's RaptorXML server supports XSLT 3.0 - no integration with Visual Studio Code is available as ALtova offer their own paid-for XML\/XSLT development solution. No XSLT standards conformance data is published for this processor."},{"page":"XSLT 4.0","section":"","url":"xslt40.html","text":"XSLT 4.0 currently comprises a set of draft proposals maintained by the W3C XSLT 4.0 Community group. DeltaXignia's XSLT\/XPath language extension provides full language support for the XSLT 4.0 and XPath 4.0 features implemented in Saxon 13, as documented here: type-aware auto-completion, checks, hover help and refactoring, as well as syntax highlighting. Note that, given the independent release cycles for Saxon and the XSLT 4.0 draft, there will be differences between the implementation and the draft specification. Some parts of the specification are likely to change significantly before it is finalized. We will endeavour to keep this extension up to date with the latest Saxon XSLT 4.0 implementation. Earlier versions of XSLT 4.0 will not be supported for auto-completion and linting."},{"page":"XSLT 4.0","section":"Editing XSLT 4.0","url":"xslt40.html#editing-xslt-4-0","text":"The version attribute on the stylesheet root element controls the XSLT version supported by the linter and for auto-completion. When the version attribute on the stylesheet root element is set to 4.0, auto-completion for XSLT instructions and attributes and XPath functions will be included. In an XSLT 3.0 stylesheet, the linter reports XPath 4.0 syntax as an error. For Saxon PE or EE 12.8 or later, run with syntax extensions, XSLT 4.0 item types - with record and enumeration types - and xsl:note can also be enabled in XSLT 3.0 stylesheets: see XSLT 4.0 Item Types and Notes in XSLT 3.0. XPath files, with the .xpath extension, are always treated as XPath 4.0."},{"page":"XSLT 4.0","section":"XSLT 4.0 support","url":"xslt40.html#xslt-4-0-support","text":"The syntax highlighter, linter and auto-completion support: New XPath 4.0 syntax - the pipeline -> and mapping arrow =!> operators, braced if expressions, map constructors without the map keyword, string templates, and the new -or-self axes New item types - choice types, such as (xs:date | xs:time), and the enum, record, fn and jnode types Named item types - declared with xsl:item-type, with checks on the names used, go to definition and find references Function syntax - fn and focus functions, keyword arguments such as ex:area(scale := 2), optional parameters (xsl:param required=\"no\") and partial function application Functions in no namespace - an xsl:function name without a prefix, e.g. name=\"double\", called as double(2) - a Saxon 13 extension, not available in Saxon-HE. A function with the name and arity of a built-in function, e.g. count, replaces it in calls without a prefix, so is reported as a warning. JNodes - path expressions on trees of maps and arrays, with jtree(), jkey(), jvalue() and get(...) steps New instructions - snippets for xsl:switch, xsl:array and xsl:select, and template rules declared within an xsl:mode (enclosed modes) Features in the XSLT 4.0 draft that Saxon 13 doesn't implement - such as extensible record types - are reported by the linter. Some of the more significant XSLT 4.0 features have their own pages: Records and Enums - auto-completion, checks and refactoring for record and enumeration types, including xsl:switch on an enumeration JSON and JNodes - path expressions on JSON, with auto-completion, checks and hover help for JNodes with a record type Code Documentation - documenting functions and templates with xsl:note, for hover help and signature help"},{"page":"XSLT 4.0","section":"Running XSLT 4.0","url":"xslt40.html#running-xslt-4-0","text":"XSLT 4.0 stylesheets run with Quick Run, or with an XSLT task. Saxon needs the --allowSyntaxExtensions:on configuration property for XPath 4.0 syntax. This is controlled by the allowSyntaxExtensions40 task property: auto (the default) - on, unless the Saxon jar file, or SaxonC path, is for Saxon-HE on - always on off - never on The free Saxon-HE version does not support the language extensions for XSLT 4.0. You will need to reference a Saxon-PE or Saxon-EE Jar file (with license file) in the saxonJar property of the task definition, or the equivalent SaxonC edition."},{"page":"XSLT 4.0","section":"Other Editors for XSLT 4.0","url":"xslt40.html#other-editors-for-xslt-4-0","text":"The Oxygen XML editor also provides language support for XSLT 4.0, with its own bundled Saxon implementation. While Oxygen does need to be purchased, it has the advantage of including Saxon-EE built-in, so you will not need to purchase Saxon separately. Oxygen XML provides an integrated solution for XML developers and content authors, and hosts an array of other features."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"","url":"xslt40-records.html","text":"XPath 4.0 adds record types, for maps with a known set of fields, and enumeration types, for strings with a known set of values. XSLT 4.0 adds the xsl:item-type declaration to give these types names. When a variable, parameter or function result is declared with one of these types, the XSLT\/XPath extension uses the declared type for auto-completion, linting, hover help and navigation - for example, to list the fields when building a map, or the values of an enumeration inside a string literal. The extension only uses declared types - from as attributes, xsl:item-type declarations and function return types. It doesn't infer types from expressions: that is left to Saxon when the stylesheet is compiled. These features are enabled when the stylesheet has version=\"4.0\". See the XSLT 4.0 page for the XSLT 4.0 features supported. For Saxon PE or EE 12.8 or later, run with syntax extensions, they can also be enabled for XSLT 3.0 stylesheets, with the setting XSLT.validation.xslt30ItemTypesAndNotes - see XSLT 4.0 Item Types and Notes in XSLT 3.0. That page also describes converting Saxon's earlier saxon:type-alias and tuple(...) syntax."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Declaring types","url":"xslt40-records.html#declaring","text":"A record type lists its fields, each with an optional type. A field name followed by ? is optional. An enumeration type lists its values as string literals: <xsl:item-type name=\"address\" as=\"record(city as xs:string, postcode? as xs:string)\"\/> <xsl:item-type name=\"person\" as=\"record(name as xs:string, age as xs:integer, address as address)\"\/> <xsl:item-type name=\"colour\" as=\"enum('red', 'green', 'blue')\"\/> A type can also be written inline in an as attribute, for example as=\"record(x as xs:double, y as xs:double)\". In an attribute value, quotes within a type can be written as references, as Saxon allows - for example as=\"enum(&quot;it's&quot;, 'is')\" or a field name quoted with &#39; - and the extension reads them as the quotes they stand for."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Auto-completion for types","url":"xslt40-records.html#auto-completion-for-types","text":"Within an as attribute, and after instance of, castable as and the other type operators, the names of declared item types are offered with the built-in types. The record(...) and enum(...) suggestions insert a snippet with placeholders for the fields or values."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Checks on declarations","url":"xslt40-records.html#checks-on-declarations","text":"An item type name that isn't declared is reported, as are duplicate declarations and a type that refers to itself (directly or through other named types). A duplicate field name in a record type is an error, as in Saxon. A duplicate value in an enumeration type is a warning, with a Remove duplicate enum value quick fix. Extensible record types, such as record(a, *), have been dropped from XPath 4.0 and are reported as an error. Go to Definition (F12) and Find All References (⇧⌥F12) work for item type names."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Building records","url":"xslt40-records.html#building-records","text":"A record is built with a map constructor, or with the xsl:map instruction. Wherever the record type is known, the extension helps you complete the map and checks it against the type."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Map constructors","url":"xslt40-records.html#map-constructors","text":"In the select attribute of a declaration with a record type, type { (or map {) and accept the suggestion to insert a complete map constructor, with an entry for each required field - when the record has optional fields, a second suggestion includes them too: <xsl:variable name=\"p\" as=\"person\" select=\"{ 'name': __TODO.name, 'age': __TODO.age, 'address': { 'city': __TODO.city } }\"\/> Each __TODO.field placeholder is reported as a warning until it's replaced with a value. A double-click selects the whole placeholder, ready to type over. After typing , at the end of an entry, the suggestions list the fields that aren't in the map yet, including optional fields."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"The xsl:map instruction","url":"xslt40-records.html#the-xsl-map-instruction","text":"Where a record is built with xsl:map, for example in the content of an xsl:variable with a record type, typing < offers an xsl:map suggestion with an xsl:map-entry for each required field (or, as a second suggestion, for all fields). Inside an existing xsl:map, the xsl:map-entry suggestions are named after the fields not yet added, for example xsl:map-entry 'age', and the key attribute offers the field names."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Checks on records","url":"xslt40-records.html#checks-on-records","text":"Missing fields - a required field that's missing is an error, with an Add missing record fields quick fix that adds an entry, with a placeholder, for each one. Unknown fields - an entry for a field that isn't in the record type is a warning. Field values - a literal value of the wrong type, such as a string for an xs:integer field, is an error. Duplicate keys - two entries with the same literal key are an error, in any map constructor or xsl:map, whether or not it has a record type."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Where the record type comes from","url":"xslt40-records.html#where-the-record-type-comes-from","text":"Maps are checked, and completed, where their type is declared: the select or content of an xsl:variable, xsl:param or xsl:with-param with an as attribute - for an xsl:with-param without one, the type of the called template's parameter is used the result of an xsl:function with an as attribute - its last xsl:sequence xsl:sequence and xsl:select with an as attribute the arguments of calls to user-defined functions, including keyword arguments such as ex:move(point := { ... }) and the left-hand operand of the arrow operators a typed let binding, such as let $p as person := { ... }"},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Using records","url":"xslt40-records.html#using-records","text":""},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Lookups","url":"xslt40-records.html#lookups","text":"After the ? lookup operator on a value with a record type, the suggestions list the record's fields. This works for variables, calls to functions with a declared record result, and chains of lookups such as $p?address?city. A lookup of a field that isn't in the record type is a warning."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Hover and navigation","url":"xslt40-records.html#hover-and-navigation","text":"Hovering over a field name - in a lookup, a map constructor key or the key of an xsl:map-entry - shows the field's declaration. Go to Definition on a field name goes to the field in the record type. Hovering over the name of a named item type - where it's used, or on its declaration - shows its declaration, with its values for an enumeration type. When the xsl:item-type has a documentation note, the note is shown too, and a field's @field text is shown when hovering over the field, and with the field in the auto-completion suggestions. Hover over a record field in a lookup, e.g. $shape?colour, showing the field's declaration and its @field text from the xsl:item-type's documentation note."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Renaming a field","url":"xslt40-records.html#rename-field","text":"Rename Symbol (F2) on a field name - in the record type, a lookup, a map constructor key, the key of an xsl:map-entry, or an @field tag of a documentation note - renames the field, and all its references. Find All References (⇧⌥F12) lists them. Only the field of that record type is renamed - not a field with the same name in another record type. The references are found in the stylesheet, the modules it includes or imports, and - from the workspace's modules - each stylesheet that imports or includes it, with the modules they use. So a rename in a module of types reaches the stylesheets that use them. A quoted field name keeps its quotes, e.g. 'unit name'. A new name must be an NCName, unless the field's name isn't one."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Path steps on records (JNodes)","url":"xslt40-records.html#path-steps-on-records-jnodes","text":"XPath 4.0 path expressions can navigate maps and arrays as trees of JNodes, e.g. jtree($p)\/address\/city. Field names are suggested, and checked, for steps after jtree($p)\/, and after a variable declared as a JNode for a record type, such as jnode(*, person). See JSON and JNodes."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Refactoring: Extract record type","url":"xslt40-records.html#record-refactoring","text":"When updating XSLT 3.0 code that builds maps, the Extract record type refactoring creates a record type from an existing map: Select a map constructor or xsl:map whose keys are string literals - or simply place the cursor on the start tag of the xsl:variable, xsl:param, xsl:with-param, xsl:function, xsl:sequence or xsl:select that it's the value of. Open the refactorings with the 💡 light-bulb, or ⌘., and choose Extract record type. An xsl:item-type declaration is added for the record type, and the declaration's as attribute is set to it - replacing a generic type such as map(*). Field types are taken from literal values, and a nested map becomes a nested record type. The new type is named record-type, and Rename Symbol is started so you can give it a meaningful name. If an existing xsl:item-type already has the same field names, a Use record type 'name' refactoring is also offered, in the Rewrite section of the list. It sets the as attribute to the existing type without adding a declaration. Before refactor: The two 💡 refactorings for a candidate record-type variable. After refactor: a prompt to rename the record-type now it's declared and set on the variable."},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Enumeration types","url":"xslt40-records.html#enums","text":""},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"Auto-completion for values","url":"xslt40-records.html#auto-completion-for-values","text":"Where an enumeration type is expected, typing a quote character offers the enumeration's values. This applies to: the select of an xsl:variable, xsl:param or xsl:with-param declared with the type (or xs:boolean, for true() and false()) arguments of calls to user-defined functions whose parameter has the type, and typed let bindings record fields with the type, in map constructors the test of an xsl:when in an xsl:switch - see below A string literal that isn't one of the values is reported as an error. Hovering over the name of an enumeration type - or a global variable or parameter, a parameter of a function or template, or a record field, with the type - lists its values - on one line for a few values, otherwise as a list. For a type declared as another, or as a choice of enumeration types, the values are those it resolves to. Hover over the name of an enumeration type, showing its values"},{"page":"Records and Enums","section":"xsl:switch on an enumeration","url":"xslt40-records.html#switch","text":"The XSLT 4.0 xsl:switch instruction selects an xsl:when by comparing a value with each test. When the select has an enumeration type - a variable, function call or record field lookup with a declared type - the extension works with its values. Quick Fix options shown for xsl:switch on an enum type. A Quick Fix options are shown on any selected xsl:switch element with a (detected) enum type: Add missing xsl:when cases with select (self-closing <xsl:when select=\"\"\/>) Add missing xsl:when cases with content ( <xsl:when>\\n<xsl:when\/>) If using auto-complete inside xsl:switch, all xsl:when options remaining for that enum are in the completion-list"},{"page":"JSON and JNodes","section":"","url":"xslt40-jnodes.html","text":"XPath 4.0 path expressions can navigate JSON - maps and arrays - as they do XML. The jtree() function wraps a map or array in a tree of JNodes, with a JNode for each map entry and array member, so a path such as $order\/customer\/name works on JSON data, with predicates, the descendant and parent axes, and the other features of path expressions. When a JNode is declared with a record type, for example jnode(*, order), the XSLT\/XPath extension knows which fields each step can reach. It checks each step, offers the field names as auto-completion suggestions, and shows each field's type and documentation on hover. JNodes need version=\"4.0\" and Saxon-PE or Saxon-EE 13, run with syntax extensions enabled (Quick Run enables these for a version=\"4.0\" stylesheet). Saxon-HE 13 doesn't support XPath 4.0 syntax. In an XSLT 3.0 stylesheet, the jnode(...) item type and the get(...) node test are reported as needing XPath 4.0. A sample project, xslt-json-schema-records, shows the features on this page in use. It converts a JSON Schema to XSLT 4.0 record types, reading the schema as JNodes, then reads JSON order data typed with the generated record types."},{"page":"JSON and JNodes","section":"Navigating JSON with JNodes","url":"xslt40-jnodes.html#navigating","text":"A JNode tree is made with jtree(), from a map or array - for example the result of json-doc() or parse-json(): <xsl:variable name=\"root\" select=\"jtree(json-doc('order.schema.json'))\"\/> <xsl:variable name=\"defs\" as=\"jnode()*\" select=\"$root\/get('$defs')\/*\"\/> Name steps select map entries by key, e.g. $order\/customer\/name. * selects every entry of a map, or every member of an array, e.g. $order\/lines\/*. get(...) is the node test for a key that isn't an NCName, such as get('$ref') or get('first name'), or for an array member by position, such as get(1). The extension reports get('a') used on its own, as a function call, as a call to an unknown function. Axes work as they do for XML: $schema\/\/get('$ref') finds every $ref at any depth, and $line\/..\/..\/currency goes up from an order line, through the lines array, to the order. jkey() gives a JNode's key - the map key, or the position of an array member - and jvalue() gives the value it wraps. Where a value is atomized, such as in an arithmetic expression or a text value template, a JNode gives its value, so $line\/quantity * $line\/unit-price works without jvalue(). A JNode is matched with the jnode() item type, e.g. $tree\/type instance of jnode(type). jnode() is offered in auto-completion for as attributes in XSLT 4.0. Saxon 13 doesn't support type node tests such as ~record(...) or ~xs:string in a path step, so the extension reports these as errors."},{"page":"JSON and JNodes","section":"JNodes with a record type","url":"xslt40-jnodes.html#typed-jnodes","text":"A variable, parameter or function result declared as jnode(*, T), where T is a record type, is a JNode whose value is a T. A named item type can be used too, e.g. <xsl:item-type name=\"order-node\" as=\"jnode(*, order)\"\/>. Child steps from the JNode are then auto-completed and checked against the record's fields, at any depth: <xsl:variable name=\"order-value\" as=\"order\" select=\"json-doc($order-uri)\"\/> <xsl:variable name=\"order\" as=\"jnode(*, order)\" select=\"jtree($order-value)\"\/> ... <xsl:function name=\"ord:line-total\" as=\"xs:double\"> <xsl:param name=\"line\" as=\"jnode(*, line)\"\/> <xsl:sequence select=\"$line\/quantity * $line\/unit-price\"\/> <\/xsl:function> Auto-completion - after a \/, the suggestions list the record's fields. Typing $order\/customer\/ lists the fields of the customer record type. Checks - a step that isn't a field is a warning, e.g. Child step 'nam' - this is not a field of the record type: customer for $order\/customer\/nam. A ? lookup on the JNode, e.g. $order?id, is checked in the same way. Hover - hovering over a field name in a step, such as unit-price in $line\/unit-price, shows the field's type. Its @field text is shown too, from the xsl:item-type's documentation note. Hovering over the type name in jnode(*, order) shows the record type, with its fields and documentation. Child steps after jtree($p)\/, where $p has a record type, are auto-completed and checked in the same way. Hovering on order in jnode(*, order) shows the record type, imported from a module of types generated from a JSON Schema: its fields, their types, and each field's description from the schema."},{"page":"JSON and JNodes","section":"Maps need jtree() before '\/'","url":"xslt40-jnodes.html#jtree-fix","text":"A \/ step needs a JNode on its left, not a map. Saxon 13 reports XPTY0019 for a step such as $order-value\/id, where $order-value is a map with a record type, so the extension reports this as an error, with the suggested fix jtree($order-value)\/id. Alternatives are a lookup, $order-value?id, or a variable declared as a jnode(*, order), as above. A step after a lookup, such as $p?address\/city, is reported in the same way."},{"page":"JSON and JNodes","section":"Debugging: xdm:debug() shows JNode paths","url":"xslt40-jnodes.html#debugging","text":"The xdm:debug() function shows a JNode's location in its tree, as an XPath path from the tree's root, above the value it wraps - in the same way it shows the location of an XML node. For an order line, this is a path such as \/lines\/*[2], so you can see which part of the JSON each value came from: <xsl:message select=\"xdm:debug('ord:line', map { 'line': $line, 'sku': $line\/sku, 'bundle': $line\/bundle-items })\"\/> The xsl:message output, for the second order line: ────────────────────────────── ord:line ────────────────────────────── line: \/lines\/*[2] { 'sku': 'KIT-7', 'description': 'Electronics starter kit', 'quantity': 1, 'unit-price': 64, 'bundle-items': [ {'sku': 'TL-1', 'description': 'Soldering iron', 'quantity': 1, 'unit-price': 0}, {'sku': 'TL-2', 'description': 'Multimeter', 'quantity': 1, 'unit-price': 0} ] } sku: \/lines\/*[2]\/sku 'KIT-7' bundle: \/lines\/*[2]\/bundle-items [ {'sku': 'TL-1', 'description': 'Soldering iron', 'quantity': 1, 'unit-price': 0}, {'sku': 'TL-2', 'description': 'Multimeter', 'quantity': 1, 'unit-price': 0} ] The xdm:debug variables auto-completion for xsl:message inserts a call like this one, with every in-scope variable. See Debugging."},{"page":"JSON and JNodes","section":"Things to know about JNodes and record types in Saxon 13","url":"xslt40-jnodes.html#limits","text":"A jnode(*, T) type matches only an instance of T. A map with keys that T doesn't declare isn't an instance, so jtree(json-doc('order.json')) doesn't match jnode(*, order) when the data has extra properties. Converting the value to the record type first - as for $order-value above - drops the extra keys, at every depth, and fails if a required field is missing or has the wrong type. No recursive item types. An xsl:item-type can't refer to itself, directly or through other types (Saxon reports SXTA0001). A field that would refer back, such as a bundle of order lines within an order line, has to be map(*) or array(map(*)) instead, so the steps after it aren't checked. JSON numbers are xs:double. json-doc() and parse-json() give every JSON number as an xs:double, so a record field for a number must be xs:double - xs:integer would reject the data. A JSON null is an empty sequence, so a nullable field needs an occurrence indicator, e.g. xs:string?. No ?? operator. Saxon 13 doesn't support the deep lookup operator, so use the descendant axis on JNodes instead, e.g. $schema\/\/get('$ref')."},{"page":"JSON and JNodes","section":"Sample: JSON Schema to record types","url":"xslt40-jnodes.html#sample","text":"The xslt-json-schema-records sample works in two stages: schema-to-records.xsl reads a JSON Schema, order.schema.json, as JNodes, and writes order-types.xsl: an xsl:item-type for the schema and for each of its $defs. Each property's description becomes an @field in the type's documentation note, so it's shown on hover. order-report.xsl includes order-types.xsl, and prints a summary of order.json, using jnode(*, order), jnode(*, line) and other typed JNodes. Run each stylesheet with Quick Run, choosing 'None' as the XML context file, so it starts from xsl:initial-template. Then, in order-report.xsl, try: typing $order\/customer\/ to see the customer's fields as suggestions changing $order\/customer\/name to $order\/customer\/nam, to see the warning hovering on unit-price in $line\/unit-price, or on line in jnode(*, line), to see the documentation taken from the schema changing $order\/id to $order-value\/id, to see the jtree() error and its fix adding a property to customer in order.schema.json, running schema-to-records.xsl again, and seeing it offered after $order\/customer\/ The sample's README describes how each JSON Schema keyword maps to an XPath type."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"","url":"xslt40-notes.html","text":"XSLT 4.0 adds the xsl:note element, for documentation within a stylesheet. The XSLT\/XPath extension supports notes written in a simple format, xdoc-md: Markdown text followed by tags such as @param and @return, in the style of Javadoc and xqDoc. A note in this format, as the first child of an xsl:function, a named xsl:template or an xsl:item-type, is shown in hover help wherever the declaration is used - including from other stylesheet modules - and on the declaration's own name. For functions and templates, it's also shown in signature help. For a record type, declared with xsl:item-type, the note can also describe each of the record's fields. A module note, as the first child of the xsl:stylesheet, describes the module and its global parameters and variables - and a global parameter or variable can have a note of its own, for more detail. The XSLT 4.0 specification allows xsl:note anywhere, with any attributes and content, and leaves the format of the documentation open. The format=\"xdoc-md\" attribute is the extension's convention for the notes it shows: XSLT processors ignore it, with the rest of the note. These features are enabled when the stylesheet has version=\"4.0\" - and for XSLT 3.0, see Notes in XSLT 3.0."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Writing a documentation note","url":"xslt40-notes.html#writing","text":"Set the format attribute to xdoc-md. The text before the first tag is a description, in Markdown. Each tag starts a new line, and continues on the following lines up to the next tag: The xdoc-md tags within xsl:note are highlighted along with any markdown used"},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Tags","url":"xslt40-notes.html#tags","text":"@param $name - a parameter of a function or template - or, in a module note, a global parameter @return - the result of a function or template @field name - a field of a record type, for an xsl:item-type @variable $name - a global variable, in a module note @see - a related function, template, item type, variable or URI - see References to other declarations @since - the version it was added in @deprecated - why it should no longer be used, and what to use instead @error - an error a function or template may raise @author and @version - for a module note @field isn't an xqDoc tag: the extension adds it, in the same style, for documenting records. A field name that isn't an NCName is quoted, as in the record type - for example @field 'unit name' e.g. cm. The xdoc-md tags and Markdown are highlighted within an xsl:note."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Markdown","url":"xslt40-notes.html#markdown","text":"The description and tag text can use Markdown: **bold**, *italics*, `code`, [links](https:\/\/example.com) and # headings. As the note is XML, the characters < and & must be escaped as normal XML, for example &lt;. Alternatively, put text containing markup in a CDATA section: its content is shown exactly as written."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Adding a note","url":"xslt40-notes.html#adding","text":"With the cursor on the start tag of an xsl:function, xsl:template, xsl:item-type, the xsl:stylesheet, or a global xsl:param or xsl:variable, that has no xsl:note, choose Add documentation note from the refactorings (💡 or ⌘.). A note is inserted as the first child, with: an @param for each parameter, and an @return for a function (or a template with an as attribute) for an xsl:item-type with a record type, an @field for each field for a module note, an @param or @variable for each global parameter or variable that has no note of its own An empty element, e.g. <xsl:item-type ...\/>, gets an end tag for the note. Press Tab to move between the description placeholders. Notes can also be added with auto-completion, after <: xsl:note inserts a note on one line. It's offered within any XSLT element, and within literal result elements - XSLT 4.0 allows xsl:note anywhere. xsl:note xdoc-md inserts a documentation note. It's offered where one is used - within a function, named template, item type, global parameter or variable, or at the top level for the module note - and when there isn't one yet. There, it's listed first, and preselected. Within the start tag of an xsl:note, format is offered as an attribute, and xdoc-md as its value. Within a note, typing @ offers the tags that apply: @field for an xsl:item-type; @param, @return and @error for a function or template; and @param, @variable, @author and @version for a module note. After @param, the parameter names without an @param yet are offered - and likewise after @field and @variable. The code action 'Add documentation note' is offered on selecting a function element."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Where notes are shown","url":"xslt40-notes.html#where-shown","text":"Hover help - on a call to the function, or on the name of an xsl:call-template, the note's description and tags are shown with the signature. On the name of an xsl:with-param, the parameter's @param text is shown. Hover help on item types - on a named item type where it's used, e.g. in an as attribute or after instance of, the note is shown with the type's declaration. Hover help on declarations - on the name of an xsl:function, named xsl:template or xsl:item-type, the note is shown just as for a use of it. On the name of an xsl:param of a function or template, the parameter's @param text is shown. Record fields - the @field text is shown in hover help on a field, e.g. r in $c?r, and with the field suggestions of auto-completion: for a lookup, a child step on a JNode, a map constructor key and an xsl:map-entry. For an item type declared as another, e.g. as=\"cx:point\", the other type's @field text is used. Variable references - on a reference to a global parameter or variable, e.g. $scale, or the name of its declaration, its type and documentation are shown - see Module notes. On a reference to a parameter of a function or template, its @param text is shown. Signature help - while typing the arguments of a function call, or the xsl:with-param children of an xsl:call-template, the parameter hints show the @param text for the current parameter. Auto-completion - the xsl:with-param suggestions within an xsl:call-template show the @param text for each parameter. Notes on functions, templates and item types in included and imported stylesheets are shown too. To see xdoc-md documentation on the function (or named template) hover over the referring code."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"References to other declarations","url":"xslt40-notes.html#references","text":"A note can refer to a function, template, item type or variable declared elsewhere - after @see, or as a Markdown code span anywhere in the note - using the XPath syntax for it: my:area#2 - the function with that arity my:area() or my:area - a function of any arity - or for my:area, else a named item type or template $scale - a parameter of the function or template the note documents, or else a global parameter or variable template my:draw - a named template For example, @see my:area#2, or Uses `$scale` and `template my:draw`. In a code span, a name without a prefix is only a reference as $name, name#2, name() or template name, as code spans are used for other text too. Navigation - Go to Definition and hover help work on a reference just as on a use of the declaration, including declarations in imported and included modules. Find references and rename - the references in notes are found, and renamed, with the declaration - e.g. renaming my:area changes @see my:area#2 too. Auto-completion - after @see, and within a code span, the declarations are suggested, written so that they refer to them: parameters and variables, e.g. $scale, functions by arity, e.g. my:area#2, then item types and templates. Hover on a reference in a note to review the referenced item's description. Auto-completion hints are triggered when adding a reference in a note."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Module notes","url":"xslt40-notes.html#module-notes","text":"A documentation note as the first child of the xsl:stylesheet (or xsl:transform or xsl:package) describes the module. Its tags can describe the global parameters and variables briefly, so they don't need notes of their own: <xsl:stylesheet ... version=\"4.0\"> <xsl:note format=\"xdoc-md\"> Draws shapes. @param $scale the scale factor @variable $origin the point all shapes start from @author Ann @version 1.2 <\/xsl:note> <xsl:param name=\"scale\" as=\"xs:double\" select=\"1\"\/> <xsl:param name=\"colour\" as=\"cx:colour\" select=\"'red'\"> <xsl:note format=\"xdoc-md\">The fill colour, used for **every** shape.<\/xsl:note> <\/xsl:param> For more detail, a global xsl:param or xsl:variable can have a documentation note of its own, as its first child. It's shown in place of the module note's @param or @variable text - so a module note can give a brief summary, and the parameter's own note the details. Hover help on variables - on a reference to a global parameter or variable, or the name of its declaration: its type, its documentation, and for an enumeration type, its values. Hover help on imports - on the href of an xsl:import or xsl:include: the module's note, and its path. Preview - on the start tag of the module note: the note as it's shown for an import of the module. Rename - renaming a global parameter or variable renames its @param or @variable tag too. The hover on a reference to a global parameter, $scale, showing its @param text from the module note. The hover on the start tag of a module note, previewing the note. The hover on the href of an xsl:import, showing the imported module's note."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Notes in XSLT 3.0","url":"xslt40-notes.html#xslt30","text":"Unless you're using a compatible Saxon version with Saxon extensions enabled (see note below), an XSLT 3.0 processor reports XTSE0010 for xsl:note, as an unknown XSLT element - the workaround is just to exclude this with use-when=\"false()\", which works with any processor. In a stylesheet with version=\"3.0\": an xsl:note without a use-when attribute is a warning, with the quick fix Exclude with use-when=\"false()\" the xsl:note completions and Add documentation note insert the note with use-when=\"false()\" documentation notes are shown, highlighted and checked, as in XSLT 4.0 Saxon PE and EE 12.8 or later, run with Saxon extensions, accept xsl:note in an XSLT 3.0 stylesheet. With the setting XSLT.validation.xslt30ItemTypesAndNotes, the linter recognises notes so they need no use-when, XSLT 4.0 xsl:item-type instructions are fully supported too - see XSLT 4.0 Item Types and Notes in XSLT 3.0."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Highlighting Details","url":"xslt40-notes.html#highlighting","text":"The content of a note is highlighted as documentation: tags, parameter and field names, code, bold and italic text, headings and links each have their own semantic token type. The token types have fallbacks, so they're highlighted in most color themes. To change their colors, add rules for the token types xdocText, xdocTag, xdocParam, xdocCode, xdocBold, xdocItalic, xdocHeading, xdocLink and xdocCdata to the editor.semanticTokenColorCustomizations setting."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Notes as XML","url":"xslt40-notes.html#xml","text":"The extension includes an Invisible XML grammar for its documentation notes, xdoc-md.ixml. With the XPath 4.0 invisible-xml() function, a stylesheet can use it to turn its notes into XML - for example, to generate documentation for a library of functions."},{"page":"Code Documentation","section":"Tag Checks","url":"xslt40-notes.html#checks","text":"The @param tags are checked against the xsl:param elements of the function or template, and the @field tags against the fields of the item type's record type: an @param for a name that isn't a parameter, or an @field for a name that isn't a field, is a warning a second @param for the same parameter, or @field for the same field, is a warning when a note has @param tags, parameters without one are reported as information, with an Add missing @param quick fix - and likewise fields without an @field, with an Add missing @field quick fix an @field in the note of a function or template, or of an item type that isn't a record type, is a warning an @param, @return or @error in the note of an xsl:item-type is a warning an @see reference that refers to no declaration, e.g. @see my:aera#2, is a warning - but not other text, e.g. a URL, or a built-in function or type, e.g. fn:sum#1 In a module note, the @param and @variable tags are checked against the global parameters and variables in the same way. A global parameter or variable with a note of its own isn't reported as missing from the module note. @return and @error in a module note, and @param, @variable, @return and @error in the note of a global parameter or variable, are warnings."}];
